00:09:54.544 --> 00:09:57.047
Random at first, but once
you start working with a customer,


00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:00.258
they'll continue to be routed over to you
for the sake of continuity.


00:10:00.342 --> 00:10:02.386
Um, you get the query,
figure out the answer,


00:10:02.469 --> 00:10:05.681
you write them back,
and that is the core of it.


00:10:05.764 --> 00:10:07.724
Simple enough in theory. So far so good?


00:10:07.808 --> 00:10:09.559
- Uh, yeah.
- Cool.


00:10:09.643 --> 00:10:12.354
So let's say you've answered
a client's question,


00:10:12.437 --> 00:10:13.605
and they seem satisfied.


00:10:13.689 --> 00:10:16.250
Now, it's not just that you get a smile
or a frown, though that is part of it.


00:10:16.274 --> 00:10:18.735
You get your smile...
it's almost always a smile.


00:10:18.819 --> 00:10:21.321
Um, that is when you send the survey.


00:10:21.405 --> 00:10:24.825
The survey is a set of quick questions
about, you know, your overall service,


00:10:24.908 --> 00:10:28.078
how you did,
and they send that immediately back,


00:10:28.161 --> 00:10:31.307
and you get to see how you did right here.
That is the rating. Uh, you know what?


00:10:31.331 --> 00:10:32.624
Let's just do a live one.


00:10:33.792 --> 00:10:35.335
- Excuse me.
- Sorry.


00:10:35.419 --> 00:10:38.088
Okay.


00:10:38.171 --> 00:10:40.424
Have a nice day.


00:10:43.593 --> 00:10:45.095
That's pretty good, 99 is good,


00:10:45.178 --> 00:10:49.182
but I can't help wondering
why it wasn't 100. Let's look.


00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:52.018
Okay.


00:10:55.897 --> 00:10:59.297
Boom. Sometimes you just encounter someone
who isn't really sensitive to the metrics.


00:10:59.359 --> 00:11:03.113
So it's important to ask them
and make sure you get that clarity.


00:11:03.196 --> 00:11:06.783
And we're back to a perfect score, yeah.


00:11:06.867 --> 00:11:08.827
Oh, wow.


00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:11.121
All right, are you ready to do this
on your own?


00:11:11.204 --> 00:11:12.414
- Yes.
- Awesome.


00:11:59.503 --> 00:12:01.063
Excuse me. I'm terribly sorry.


00:12:02.047 --> 00:12:03.048
Hey, how's it going?


00:12:03.131 --> 00:12:04.859
- I thought you were in Amsterdam.
- Oh, just for a day.


00:12:04.883 --> 00:12:06.402
Then London, then New York, now here.


00:12:06.426 --> 00:12:08.637
- Have you slept?
- A few hours on the plane.


00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:11.014
A couple pills, I'm good.
So you? First week.


00:12:11.097 --> 00:12:13.141
What?


00:12:13.225 --> 00:12:14.309
You got an 87 average.


00:12:14.392 --> 00:12:16.686
- That's like a solid B.
- How long till I'm fired?


00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:18.789
Listen, when I first started,
it took me a few weeks


00:12:18.813 --> 00:12:20.374
to really get the hang of it.
Believe me, okay?


00:12:20.398 --> 00:12:22.609
Believe me, you're doing fine.
You're good, all right?


00:12:22.692 --> 00:12:23.693
Mm-hm.


00:12:23.777 --> 00:12:24.861
Hello, I'm Eamon Bailey.


00:12:28.114 --> 00:12:29.449
Hey.


00:12:33.245 --> 00:12:36.289
Thank you, thank you.
So glad to see you all here.


00:12:36.373 --> 00:12:39.876
Now a bunch of you are new
to the company since I last spoke,


00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.087
so can the guppies all stand up?


00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:45.340
- Right, that's you.
- Come on, guppies, just stand up.


00:12:45.423 --> 00:12:46.863
- Whoo!
- Guppies, stand up, come on.


00:12:48.176 --> 00:12:49.719
Congratulations, nice to have you.


00:12:49.803 --> 00:12:54.516
Now "guppies" is just an affectionate term
for you baby fishies.


00:12:54.599 --> 00:12:57.435
Soon you will all grow
to become powerful...


00:12:57.519 --> 00:12:59.938
- You're adorable.
- I hate you.


00:13:00.021 --> 00:13:02.983
...the fish that guppies grow up
into being...


00:13:03.066 --> 00:13:05.193
What do they grow up into?


00:13:05.277 --> 00:13:07.237
Anyway, all right, this is Dream Friday,


00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.116
the latest in our long tradition
of presenting our newest innovations.


00:13:11.199 --> 00:13:14.202
The first Dream Friday
featured Ty Lafitte,


00:13:14.286 --> 00:13:16.830
the inventor of True You
and our founding father.


00:13:16.913 --> 00:13:20.333
And usually, we feature one
of our engineers or our visionaries


00:13:20.417 --> 00:13:23.003
but today unfortunately,
it's just me and for that,


00:13:23.086 --> 00:13:24.671
I apologize in advance.


00:13:24.754 --> 00:13:26.524
We love you, Eamon!


00:13:26.548 --> 00:13:27.716
Thanks, Mom.


00:13:30.176 --> 00:13:31.737
Now you might not have seen me
around campus


00:13:31.761 --> 00:13:33.322
for the last few days,
and maybe you were wondering


00:13:33.346 --> 00:13:34.806
just where the heck I've been.


00:13:34.889 --> 00:13:36.016
Surfing!


00:13:36.099 --> 00:13:37.851
Argh!


00:13:37.934 --> 00:13:41.313
You got me! Yes, I've been surfing.


00:13:41.396 --> 00:13:43.732
And that's part
of what I'm here to talk about.


00:13:43.815 --> 00:13:46.151
I love to surf and in the past,


00:13:46.234 --> 00:13:50.905
I would have to wake up
and call the local surf shop


00:13:50.989 --> 00:13:54.159
to ask about the local breaks,
and pretty soon,


00:13:54.242 --> 00:13:57.454
the local surf shop
stopped answering their phone.


00:13:57.537 --> 00:14:01.708
And then I got a cell phone,


00:14:01.791 --> 00:14:04.461
and I could call up my buddies
who were already out


00:14:04.544 --> 00:14:06.713
at the beach before me,
and I could ask them,


00:14:06.796 --> 00:14:10.133
but they, too,
stopped answering their phones.


00:14:11.217 --> 00:14:12.636
And then came...


00:14:13.678 --> 00:14:14.679
the Internet.


00:14:14.763 --> 00:14:17.682
Whoa. Oh!


00:14:17.766 --> 00:14:21.686
Some geniuses set up cameras
on the beaches,


00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:25.357
so I could log on
and get some very crude images


00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:27.442
of the waves at Stinson Beach.


00:14:27.525 --> 00:14:31.237
It was almost worse
than calling the surf shop.


00:14:31.321 --> 00:14:33.114
Here's how it used to look.


00:14:34.699 --> 00:14:36.868
Almost useless, right?


00:14:36.951 --> 00:14:39.162
But now let's refresh that page


00:14:39.245 --> 00:14:43.291
and show the site
with our new Circle video delivery.


00:14:44.459 --> 00:14:47.337
Now that looks pretty good, doesn't it?


00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:49.422
Yeah, maybe I should be
out there right now.


00:14:49.506 --> 00:14:51.841
But would it surprise you to know


00:14:51.925 --> 00:14:56.221
that this is not coming
from some big camera


00:14:56.304 --> 00:14:59.391
but actually just one of these?


00:15:00.767 --> 00:15:04.396
This camera needs no wires.


00:15:04.479 --> 00:15:08.191
It transmits this image via satellite.


00:15:08.274 --> 00:15:10.443
I set up that camera this morning.


00:15:10.527 --> 00:15:14.322
I stuck it to a tree near the dunes
with no permit, nothing,


00:15:14.406 --> 00:15:16.574
in fact, no one even knows it's there.


00:15:16.658 --> 00:15:18.576
And actually,
I was pretty busy this morning


00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:23.248
because here's Rodeo Beach,
Montera,


00:15:23.331 --> 00:15:25.542
Ocean Beach,


00:15:25.625 --> 00:15:27.127
Fort Point.


00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:28.294
This is my front yard.


00:15:29.629 --> 00:15:31.881
And here's my backyard.


00:15:31.965 --> 00:15:33.133
Here's traffic on the 101.


00:15:33.216 --> 00:15:35.343
Oh, did you think this was just a camera?


00:15:35.427 --> 00:15:38.596
No, welcome
to real time analytic processing.


00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:42.350
When you see this,
you're also getting this.


00:15:45.228 --> 00:15:48.481
Air quality, traffic volume,
weather patterns, biometrics,


00:15:48.565 --> 00:15:53.570
facial recognition, saved and searchable,
all to serve you better.


00:15:53.653 --> 00:15:58.783
My friends, we are looking
at retailing these in just a few months


00:15:58.867 --> 00:16:01.286
for less than a pair of jeans.


00:16:01.369 --> 00:16:04.414
And I'm not talking about fancy jeans.


00:16:04.497 --> 00:16:07.292
I'm talking about the ones
like I'm wearing.


00:16:07.375 --> 00:16:08.418
Wow!


00:16:08.501 --> 00:16:13.298
Now, Circlers... do you like to share?


00:16:13.381 --> 00:16:15.091
Sharing is caring!


00:16:15.175 --> 00:16:18.011
Sharing is caring.
I thought you might say that.


00:16:18.094 --> 00:16:19.572
- Sharing is caring?
- Sharing is caring.


00:16:19.596 --> 00:16:24.809
I have a dozen helpers all over the world
right now carrying these cameras.


00:16:24.893 --> 00:16:27.687
Imagine the human rights implications.


00:16:27.771 --> 00:16:32.067
Activists no longer have
to hold up a camera.


00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:35.070
Now it's as easy as
gluing a camera to a wall,


00:16:35.153 --> 00:16:37.739
and actually, we've done just that.


00:16:39.074 --> 00:16:40.575
Do you see any cameras here?


00:16:41.618 --> 00:16:42.744
No.


00:16:42.827 --> 00:16:48.291
We have 144 cameras in place here,
and it took us just a day to do it,


00:16:48.374 --> 00:16:49.709
and they're virtually invisible.


00:16:49.793 --> 00:16:53.713
They come in every color,
so camouflaging is a cinch.


00:16:53.797 --> 00:16:55.965
This is my favorite. Whoa!


00:16:56.049 --> 00:16:58.259
Nice catch.


00:16:58.343 --> 00:16:59.636
Sign her up.


00:16:59.719 --> 00:17:04.516
So now, the entire world is watching.


00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:06.184
You know, I agree with the Hague,


00:17:06.267 --> 00:17:08.978
with the human rights activists
all over the world.


00:17:09.062 --> 00:17:11.022
There needs to be accountability.


00:17:11.106 --> 00:17:14.901
Tyrants and terrorists can no longer hide.


00:17:14.984 --> 00:17:19.948
We will see them. We will hear them.
We will hear and see everything.


00:17:20.031 --> 00:17:22.450
If it happens, we'll know.


00:17:24.077 --> 00:17:27.747
We're calling it... SeeChange.


00:17:29.874 --> 00:17:32.210
SeeChange.


00:17:34.546 --> 00:17:36.965
Oh, we'll know the good things, too.


00:17:37.048 --> 00:17:39.843
- Aw.
- Aw.


00:17:39.926 --> 00:17:42.178
We will see it all.


00:17:42.262 --> 00:17:45.390
Because knowing is good.


00:17:45.473 --> 00:17:47.976
But knowing everything is better.


00:17:55.275 --> 00:17:57.819
Whoo! Yeah!


00:18:06.703 --> 00:18:09.539
Good thing you guys like
to keep things modest.


00:18:09.622 --> 00:18:10.957
What's this party even for?


00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:13.751
SeeChange. Summer solstice.
Everything. Nothing.


00:18:13.835 --> 00:18:16.296
Come on, let's be the only ones
who know how to dance!


00:18:16.379 --> 00:18:18.840
My God, is that Beck?


00:18:22.218 --> 00:18:26.306
<i>♪ Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh ♪</i>


00:18:26.389 --> 00:18:29.642
<i>♪ Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh ♪</i>


00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:34.689
<i>♪ Come on out of your dreams ♪</i>


00:18:34.772 --> 00:18:37.400
<i>♪ And wake up from your rêverie ♪</i>


00:18:39.235 --> 00:18:42.947
<i>♪ Time is here, don't go to sleep ♪</i>


00:18:43.031 --> 00:18:45.700
<i>♪ Streets are running on the brink ♪</i>


00:18:46.951 --> 00:18:49.454
So Bailey, what's he actually like?


00:18:49.537 --> 00:18:50.955
You're in the gang of 40.


00:18:51.039 --> 00:18:53.350
Yeah, but I don't get a ton
of alone time with him.


00:18:53.374 --> 00:18:55.686
I mean, you know,
a lot of people work in the White House.


00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.962
How many people really get
to know the President?


00:18:58.046 --> 00:19:01.090
Oh, actually,
do you wanna see something?


00:19:01.174 --> 00:19:03.218
Oh, my God. You need to see this.
Follow me.


00:19:03.301 --> 00:19:06.262
<i>♪ Here we are ♪</i>


00:19:08.389 --> 00:19:11.017
Here. This is it.


00:19:15.855 --> 00:19:17.899
Nice knocker.


00:19:17.982 --> 00:19:21.194
Holy shit, right?


00:19:22.195 --> 00:19:24.239
Right?


00:19:24.322 --> 00:19:27.158
I think all of these have gotta
be first editions?


00:19:27.242 --> 00:19:30.161
Oh, but, um, you have to give me a verbal
nondisclosure agreement, okay?


00:19:30.245 --> 00:19:31.704
- Annie.
- Seriously.


00:19:31.788 --> 00:19:35.541
You shouldn't be here and probably
won't ever be here again, so verbal NDA.


00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:40.964
- I promise not to disclose anything.
- Good.


00:19:41.047 --> 00:19:44.092
This is where Bailey and Stenton
make all of their plans.


00:19:44.175 --> 00:19:48.721
It's very serious.
I mean, this is high nerd, right?


00:19:48.805 --> 00:19:50.525
Oh, no, you probably shouldn't play with...


00:19:52.767 --> 00:19:56.771
- Like this?
- You're such a dork.


00:19:56.854 --> 00:19:57.855
Well...


00:19:58.982 --> 00:20:02.527
- So this is where it all happens.
- To be a fly on the wall, right?


00:20:03.945 --> 00:20:05.446
We should get back to the party.


00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:07.949
Well, you should at least.


00:20:09.993 --> 00:20:12.412
- You're not coming back?
- I've got work.


00:20:12.495 --> 00:20:15.057
- Annie, it's like, 11 o'clock.
- I've got Australia in a few minutes.


00:20:15.081 --> 00:20:17.917
Ah. A-ha. Actually, a few minutes ago,
but you have fun, okay?


00:20:18.001 --> 00:20:20.295
- Annie...
- You're gonna have so much fun.


00:20:24.632 --> 00:20:26.712
- You guys are gonna love this.
- Okay.


00:20:30.054 --> 00:20:31.054
Whoops.


00:20:42.358 --> 00:20:46.029
So I heard there's a party around here.


00:20:46.112 --> 00:20:48.448
You know, if that's, like...


00:20:48.531 --> 00:20:50.241
your kind of thing.


00:20:50.325 --> 00:20:51.784
Yeah, maybe I'll check it out.


00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:56.631
It's probably not as interesting
as whatever's on your phone, though.


00:20:59.625 --> 00:21:00.835
Okay, probably not.


00:21:06.924 --> 00:21:10.261
- Do you want a drink?
- I do, but they ran out like an hour ago.


00:21:10.345 --> 00:21:12.764
I think they did too, but shh.


00:21:16.351 --> 00:21:18.936
Are you serious? Whoa.


00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:22.315
- You have glasses in there, too?
- It's my party trick.


00:21:24.817 --> 00:21:27.153
- Oh.
- All right.


00:21:28.363 --> 00:21:29.363
Thank you.


00:21:33.993 --> 00:21:34.993
You're new here.


00:21:36.371 --> 00:21:39.624
- You are, right?
- How can you tell that?


00:21:39.707 --> 00:21:42.107
- I don't know, I just guessed.
- That's really embarrassing.


00:21:43.544 --> 00:21:45.380
I'm sorry.


00:21:45.463 --> 00:21:47.340
What do you think?


00:21:47.423 --> 00:21:49.342
- Of this?
- Yeah.


00:21:52.303 --> 00:21:54.305
I'm excited.


00:21:54.389 --> 00:21:55.515
Mm-hm.


00:21:58.142 --> 00:22:00.395
- I'm sorry.
- Okay.


00:22:00.478 --> 00:22:01.997
It's just everyone
that works at the Circle


00:22:02.021 --> 00:22:04.982
is so excited to be here,


00:22:05.066 --> 00:22:08.945
and they just want to tell everyone
about it all the time. It's, you know...


00:22:09.028 --> 00:22:10.548
It's like, "Here, try this Kool-Aid!"


00:22:10.613 --> 00:22:12.466
- Oh, Kool-Aid?
- "It's delicious. You'll love it."


00:22:12.490 --> 00:22:14.158
- Oh, wow!
- I love Kool-Aid.


00:22:14.242 --> 00:22:16.035
Don't you love Kool-Aid?


00:22:17.245 --> 00:22:20.456
After my last job
where I just tried to, like,


00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:22.780
calm down angry people all day...


00:22:23.793 --> 00:22:25.795
I'm just grateful to be here.


00:22:25.878 --> 00:22:28.714
I got to go put out a fire.


00:22:28.798 --> 00:22:32.343
- Sounds exciting.
- Very. So, uh...


00:22:32.427 --> 00:22:33.428
I'll leave you to it.


00:22:35.304 --> 00:22:38.349
Have a drink, have a good time,
and stay excited.


00:22:38.433 --> 00:22:39.559
You're at the Circle.


00:22:46.107 --> 00:22:48.985
Did he tell me his name? I forgot it.


00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:54.198
- Have a good night, guppy.
- Oh! Thanks.


00:22:54.282 --> 00:22:57.201
<i>Last shuttle
will be leaving in five minutes.</i>


00:23:24.395 --> 00:23:28.191
I'm telling you Mae-Mae...
there's something about you.


00:23:28.274 --> 00:23:30.067
You have a glow.


00:23:30.151 --> 00:23:33.196
- You do.
- I do not have a glow.


00:23:33.279 --> 00:23:36.282
Like you're with child.


00:23:36.365 --> 00:23:38.117
Ignore him.


00:23:38.201 --> 00:23:40.077
So it's been great?


00:23:40.161 --> 00:23:42.205
I don't think I ever wanna work
anywhere else.


00:23:43.206 --> 00:23:45.082
I told, um, Carolina's mom.


00:23:45.166 --> 00:23:47.376
I might've let your salary slip.


00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:49.045
- Oh, Mom.
- What?


00:23:49.128 --> 00:23:53.132
It's been fun to casually
insert it into conversation.


00:23:53.216 --> 00:23:57.220
"My daughter works for the best company
on the planet!


00:23:57.303 --> 00:23:59.305
And has full dental."


00:23:59.388 --> 00:24:01.682
Please don't. I just got lucky.


00:24:01.766 --> 00:24:04.227
- If it wasn't for Annie...
- Annie, how is Annie?


00:24:05.353 --> 00:24:06.354
She's good.


00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:10.107
- Working too hard.
- What does she do exactly?


00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:13.444
She's in the gang of 40.


00:24:13.527 --> 00:24:15.529
Uh-huh.


00:24:15.613 --> 00:24:17.156
She's in on all the big decisions,


00:24:18.658 --> 00:24:20.952
deals with regulatory issues
in other countries.


00:24:22.078 --> 00:24:23.913
That sounds important.


00:24:25.539 --> 00:24:30.086
Mae, hon, why don't you get your daddy
another beer?


00:24:30.169 --> 00:24:31.170
Sure, Dad.


00:24:47.103 --> 00:24:48.854
Your hair.


00:24:48.938 --> 00:24:50.898
It was a phase.


00:24:50.982 --> 00:24:52.692
It was a cute phase.


00:24:53.943 --> 00:24:55.695
You good?


00:24:55.778 --> 00:24:57.571
I'm good.


00:24:57.655 --> 00:24:59.615
You should come visit me at work sometime.


00:24:59.699 --> 00:25:00.699
At the Circle?


00:25:01.617 --> 00:25:03.327
I'll give that idea a smile.


00:25:03.411 --> 00:25:04.870
Hey...


00:25:04.954 --> 00:25:07.581
They might think I was there
to clean the toilets.


00:25:07.665 --> 00:25:10.418
Come on. They'd love you.


00:25:10.501 --> 00:25:13.063
All those tech guys secretly wish
they knew how to use a hand saw,


00:25:13.087 --> 00:25:14.964
make something out of wood.


00:25:15.047 --> 00:25:17.133
That really sums me up pretty well.


00:25:17.216 --> 00:25:20.553
- It could be worse, I guess.
- You know what I mean.


00:25:20.636 --> 00:25:23.097
They want you to tell them
about your secret hiking spots.


00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:26.684
So they could put them on True You
and ruin it forever?


00:25:26.767 --> 00:25:29.520
Ooh, biting commentary.


00:25:29.603 --> 00:25:31.564
I mean, come on,
you have to admit, like,


00:25:31.647 --> 00:25:35.067
smiles and frowns
used to be for middle school.


00:25:35.151 --> 00:25:37.432
Like, "Hey, you know,
do you like stickers and unicorns?"


00:25:37.486 --> 00:25:40.323
"Yes, I love stickers and unicorns."
Smile, you know.


00:25:40.406 --> 00:25:42.491
And now... now it's everyone.


00:25:42.575 --> 00:25:46.454
I can't help it if you're not... social.


00:25:46.537 --> 00:25:50.541
I am social enough.
I mean, isn't... this socializing?


00:25:52.084 --> 00:25:56.339
I assume you've heard about that senator
that's trying to, like, break you guys up


00:25:56.422 --> 00:25:59.050
- citing antitrust laws, right?
- Give me a break, Mercer.


00:25:59.133 --> 00:26:01.510
Don't you have to go
and chop some wood or something?


00:26:06.557 --> 00:26:08.142
- Thanks, hon.
- Sure, Dad.


00:26:10.227 --> 00:26:11.937
His business is doing well.


00:26:12.021 --> 00:26:15.441
Mercer? The antler chandeliers?


00:26:15.524 --> 00:26:17.902
They are beautiful, Mae.
He's making one for us.


00:26:17.985 --> 00:26:22.114
Did I tell you I'm up to an 87 so far?
They say that's pretty good for a guppy.


00:26:23.532 --> 00:26:25.284
Who's a guppy?


00:26:25.368 --> 00:26:27.078
Well, that's great, Mae.


00:26:29.789 --> 00:26:32.041
- Honey?
- Hm?


00:26:32.124 --> 00:26:34.960
Oh. Come on,
we'll go in the house, come on.


00:26:35.044 --> 00:26:36.921
Dad? Don't worry.


00:26:37.004 --> 00:26:38.857
- What happened?
- Please, Mae, let your mother.


00:26:38.881 --> 00:26:39.882
Honey, just, just...


00:26:39.965 --> 00:26:41.651
Just tell everybody
that Dad's not feeling well,


00:26:41.675 --> 00:26:44.553
and they'll understand, really.
It's okay. It's okay. It's okay.


00:26:44.637 --> 00:26:46.764
It's okay.


00:26:46.847 --> 00:26:48.349
Dad.


00:26:48.432 --> 00:26:50.393
Please, Mae.


00:26:50.476 --> 00:26:52.770
We got it.


00:26:53.813 --> 00:26:54.980
It's okay.


00:26:56.732 --> 00:26:57.942
Come on.


00:27:44.989 --> 00:27:45.989
Thanks.


00:27:51.871 --> 00:27:53.038
See you soon, Mae.


00:27:54.331 --> 00:27:55.332
Bye.


00:28:17.188 --> 00:28:21.108
<i>Today, I call upon
the Senate Antitrust Subcommittee</i>


00:28:21.192 --> 00:28:22.985
<i>to investigate the Circle.</i>


00:28:23.068 --> 00:28:27.323
<i>We cannot rely on them to say,
"We're not violating antitrust laws."</i>


00:28:27.406 --> 00:28:30.576
<i>I'm sure the Circle
will be glad to cooperate.</i>


00:28:30.659 --> 00:28:34.497
<i>We have every confidence
that Senator Williamson's allegations</i>


00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:35.581
<i>will be discredited.</i>


00:28:35.664 --> 00:28:38.042
You are probably wondering
about your new screen.


00:28:38.125 --> 00:28:39.877
Hi, I'm Gina.


00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:41.253
- I'm Matt.
- Hi.


00:28:41.337 --> 00:28:44.298
Dan said this would be a good moment
to set up all your socials.


00:28:44.381 --> 00:28:45.799
- Do you have time?
- Sure.


00:28:45.883 --> 00:28:46.884
Great.


00:28:46.967 --> 00:28:51.305
I take it last week you were too busy
to set up your company social account,


00:28:51.388 --> 00:28:53.724
and I don't think
you've imported your old profile.


00:28:53.807 --> 00:28:56.018
Sorry, I've just been
kind of overwhelmed so far.


00:28:56.101 --> 00:28:57.895
- Oh.
- No, in a good way.


00:28:57.978 --> 00:29:01.815
I just haven't had time yet
for extracurricular stuff.


00:29:01.899 --> 00:29:03.317
Wow.


00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:05.486
That's so interesting you put it that way.


00:29:05.569 --> 00:29:08.531
We actually see your profile
and the activity on it


00:29:08.614 --> 00:29:12.701
as integral to your participation here.
This is how your coworkers,


00:29:12.785 --> 00:29:16.247
even the ones on the other side of campus,
know who you are.


00:29:16.330 --> 00:29:19.667
Communication certainly
isn't extracurricular, right?


00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:21.585
Right. Of course.


00:29:21.669 --> 00:29:26.048
We consider you a full knowable person
of unlimited potential


00:29:26.131 --> 00:29:28.259
and a crucial member of the community.


00:29:28.342 --> 00:29:30.970
But you're such an enigma.


00:29:31.053 --> 00:29:32.054
I am?


00:29:32.137 --> 00:29:35.808
It's been years since someone
so shrouded in mystery started here.


00:29:35.891 --> 00:29:38.519
- Everyone really likes you.
- Your work's been exemplary.


00:29:38.602 --> 00:29:40.729
Strong ratings for your first week.


00:29:40.813 --> 00:29:43.148
Are you satisfied with your performance?


00:29:43.232 --> 00:29:45.734
Uh, I think I can do better.


00:29:45.818 --> 00:29:48.487
Good, but it's not all about work, okay?


00:29:48.571 --> 00:29:51.532
- It's all also about community.
- Of course.


00:29:51.615 --> 00:29:54.034
It's all connected.


00:29:55.035 --> 00:29:58.789
But you've had a blip or two when it comes
to meshing with the community,


00:29:58.873 --> 00:30:02.251
like your absence at several weekend
or evening events.


00:30:02.334 --> 00:30:04.670
Which are, of course, optional.


00:30:04.753 --> 00:30:06.773
- I'm sorry?
- Let's start with this weekend.


00:30:06.797 --> 00:30:13.095
You left campus at 11:42 p.m. on Friday
and got back 8:46 a.m., Monday.


00:30:13.178 --> 00:30:14.680
Was there work on the weekend?


00:30:14.763 --> 00:30:16.307
- Oh, no, no!
- I didn't... I'm sorry.


00:30:16.390 --> 00:30:17.224
Oh, so sweet.


00:30:17.308 --> 00:30:19.028
There wasn't mandatory work
on the weekend.


00:30:19.059 --> 00:30:22.646
This isn't like a clock in,
clock out type place, thank God!


00:30:22.730 --> 00:30:24.541
But, you know,
there were thousands of people here


00:30:24.565 --> 00:30:26.459
participating in a hundred
different activities.


00:30:26.483 --> 00:30:27.483
So many.


00:30:29.570 --> 00:30:31.655
I'm sorry, I, um...


00:30:31.739 --> 00:30:35.701
My dad had an... an episode,
and I was home helping out with that.


00:30:35.784 --> 00:30:38.454
I'm so sorry to hear that.
Is that related to his MS?


00:30:40.164 --> 00:30:42.875
Uh... yeah.


00:30:42.958 --> 00:30:44.718
We didn't know anything
about this incident.


00:30:44.793 --> 00:30:47.087
Did you reach out to any Circlers
during this crisis?


00:30:47.171 --> 00:30:51.383
- Annie? Anybody?
- Uh, no. I... No.


00:30:51.467 --> 00:30:55.512
Mae, there are four groups here
for staffers dealing with MS,


00:30:55.596 --> 00:30:57.681
two of them for children of MS patients.


00:30:57.765 --> 00:31:00.017
Have you sought out a group?


00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:02.645
- I didn't know.
- Okay.


00:31:02.728 --> 00:31:06.815
Can you... talk about what you did
the rest of the weekend?


00:31:06.899 --> 00:31:09.485
Nothing, really. I just... I kayaked.


00:31:09.568 --> 00:31:12.738
You kayaked? With who?


00:31:12.821 --> 00:31:15.407
Nobody. Just on my own.


00:31:15.491 --> 00:31:16.992
I kayak.


00:31:17.076 --> 00:31:18.303
We could have kayaked together.


00:31:18.327 --> 00:31:23.374
Nothing on your profile about kayaking,
no smiles, no ratings, no posts.


00:31:23.457 --> 00:31:27.878
I'm no psychologist, but this behavior
sometimes stems from low self-worth.


00:31:27.962 --> 00:31:32.466
This is what participation is all about,
bringing you, lifting you up.


00:31:32.549 --> 00:31:35.803
Thus, your participation window.


00:31:35.886 --> 00:31:39.390
I can't believe you've been here this long
without being on the main social feed.


00:31:39.473 --> 00:31:41.600
You're about to have your world rocked.


00:31:42.685 --> 00:31:44.645
See, these are all
of last week's messages.


00:31:44.728 --> 00:31:46.355
That's why there's so many.


00:31:46.438 --> 00:31:49.441
- Eight thousand?
- Mm-hm.


00:31:49.525 --> 00:31:52.945
Looks like you have a lot
of catching up to do here. A feast!


00:31:53.028 --> 00:31:54.571
Have fun.


00:31:56.073 --> 00:31:57.908
Oh, forgot one more thing.


00:31:57.992 --> 00:32:01.829
This is your participation rank.
Party rank for short.


00:32:01.912 --> 00:32:05.958
Some people here call it the
popularity rank, but it's not really that.


00:32:06.041 --> 00:32:07.960
It's just an algorithm-generated number


00:32:08.043 --> 00:32:10.963
that takes into account
all your activity in the inner circle.


00:32:11.046 --> 00:32:12.046
That makes sense?


00:32:12.965 --> 00:32:16.510
- I think so.
- Okay. And again, it's just for fun.


00:32:16.593 --> 00:32:18.554
- It's just for fun!
- Okay, bye.


00:32:21.849 --> 00:32:24.268
Um, I think
it's for Tuesday next week?


00:32:24.351 --> 00:32:26.020
Is that in Building 74 or...?


00:32:26.103 --> 00:32:28.343
- Uh, no. It's actually off campus.
- Okay.


00:33:08.687 --> 00:33:09.687
<i>It's been a month.</i>


00:33:09.730 --> 00:33:12.483
<i>When are you coming home?
We miss you.</i>


00:33:12.566 --> 00:33:14.276
I can't. You know.


00:33:14.359 --> 00:33:17.237
<i>How's the dorm?
Do you have to clean your own room?</i>


00:33:17.321 --> 00:33:19.615
- We have people that do that.
- <i>Ooh, thank God.</i>


00:33:19.698 --> 00:33:21.784
How's Dad?


00:33:21.867 --> 00:33:24.828
<i>He's okay. He... he fell yesterday.</i>


00:33:24.912 --> 00:33:28.415
<i>And I couldn't lift him up,
but thank God Mercer came by.</i>


00:33:28.499 --> 00:33:29.708
<i>He's stronger than I thought.</i>


00:33:31.418 --> 00:33:32.419
I'm sorry.


00:33:33.504 --> 00:33:34.630
I should have been there.


00:33:43.097 --> 00:33:44.181
Is that one of his?


00:33:45.182 --> 00:33:49.019
<i>- Yeah. I think it's his best work.</i>
- It's awesome.


00:33:51.355 --> 00:33:54.650
I gotta go, Mom. I love you.


00:33:54.733 --> 00:33:56.819
<i>- Okay, well, bye. Bye.</i>
- Bye.


00:34:12.793 --> 00:34:16.421
I'm so sorry about last week.
I've been up my own ass.


00:34:16.505 --> 00:34:18.358
And I didn't mean
to be a shitty friend.


00:34:18.382 --> 00:34:20.843
It's okay. Where are we going?


00:34:20.926 --> 00:34:23.387
Listen, I've been looking
into your parents' situation,


00:34:23.470 --> 00:34:26.431
and I think... I have an idea.


00:34:26.515 --> 00:34:28.350
All right? Yeah?


00:34:28.433 --> 00:34:29.601
- Hi.
- Hi.


00:34:29.685 --> 00:34:33.480
- This is Jessica. This is Mae.
- Hello, Mae, how are you?


00:34:33.564 --> 00:34:36.108
I understand you haven't done
your medical intake yet.


00:34:36.191 --> 00:34:38.443
- No.
- Can you drink this for me?


00:34:43.615 --> 00:34:44.908
All of it, if you can.


00:34:49.746 --> 00:34:53.542
- I told you about Mae's dad's...
- Mm-hm, multiple sclerosis.


00:34:53.625 --> 00:34:56.670
I did my post doc on MS.
Love to get some real time data.


00:34:57.963 --> 00:34:59.006
Here you go.


00:35:00.048 --> 00:35:01.048
Okay.


00:35:02.718 --> 00:35:05.012
- How does this feel?
- Warm.


00:35:06.972 --> 00:35:09.391
Is this for free?


00:35:09.474 --> 00:35:11.310
Of course. You get to keep it.


00:35:11.393 --> 00:35:13.228
So, it's going to feel warm
for a few days,


00:35:13.312 --> 00:35:15.606
then you and the media band
will get used to each other,


00:35:15.689 --> 00:35:18.692
but it has to touch the skin to work.


00:35:18.775 --> 00:35:21.445
Waterproof. No need to take it off.


00:35:21.528 --> 00:35:23.155
Let's activate it, shall we?


00:35:25.032 --> 00:35:25.866
Is that the...?


00:35:25.949 --> 00:35:29.369
Well, it's syncing up with the sensor
you ingested a second ago.


00:35:32.789 --> 00:35:34.541
And now...


00:35:34.625 --> 00:35:36.865
Let's have you walk around the room.


00:35:41.131 --> 00:35:42.131
Briskly.


00:35:43.926 --> 00:35:47.262
It'll collect data on your heart rate,
blood pressure, cholesterol,


00:35:47.346 --> 00:35:53.060
sleep duration, sleep quality,
digestive efficiency, on and on.


00:35:53.143 --> 00:35:55.938
And all that data is stored
in the Cloud and in your tablet,


00:35:56.021 --> 00:35:59.524
anywhere you want, and of course, it's all
collated with the rest of the Circle.


00:36:00.692 --> 00:36:02.212
You'd be amazed at the patterns we see


00:36:02.236 --> 00:36:04.863
and the problems we can avoid
when we have all that data.


00:36:04.947 --> 00:36:07.342
Speaking of which, I was thinking
it would make sense for...


00:36:07.366 --> 00:36:10.077
Mae's folks to come
on the Circle health plan.


00:36:10.160 --> 00:36:11.787
Uh, wait, what?


00:36:11.870 --> 00:36:14.349
Wouldn't be the first time,
and I'd really like to get


00:36:14.373 --> 00:36:15.582
real time data from your dad.


00:36:15.666 --> 00:36:17.000
Sit back down.


00:36:17.084 --> 00:36:18.794
Can you get in touch with HR,


00:36:18.877 --> 00:36:20.021
- clear it with them?
- Yep.


00:36:20.045 --> 00:36:21.755
- Tell them I say it's a go.
- Absolutely.


00:36:23.590 --> 00:36:26.009
We'll send someone out
to set them up, okay?


00:36:28.136 --> 00:36:30.222
<i>Honey, this is gonna
save our lives.</i>


00:36:30.305 --> 00:36:33.892
<i>- Thank you. And thank Annie too.</i>
- They thank you.


00:36:33.976 --> 00:36:35.477
Aw.


00:36:35.560 --> 00:36:37.980
<i>This is amazing. Call you later, okay?</i>


00:36:38.063 --> 00:36:40.899
Okay. I gotta go.
Big event here on campus.


00:36:40.983 --> 00:36:42.150
<i>- Exciting.</i>
- I know.


00:36:42.234 --> 00:36:44.027
<i>- I love you.</i>
- I love you, too.


00:36:44.111 --> 00:36:46.488
- Bye, Mom!
- Bye.


00:36:47.948 --> 00:36:51.535
- Thank you.
- Oh. You're welcome.


00:36:53.453 --> 00:36:55.622
All right. You okay?


00:36:55.706 --> 00:36:57.916
Okay. Come on.


00:37:05.132 --> 00:37:09.511
Openness is something
we advocate here at the Circle.


00:37:10.554 --> 00:37:11.555
We live it.


00:37:11.638 --> 00:37:15.392
We demonstrate our commitment
to openness every day.


00:37:16.977 --> 00:37:20.147
Our government, not so much.


00:37:20.230 --> 00:37:24.026
We expect openness,
and we get obfuscation.


00:37:24.109 --> 00:37:25.902
We get doublespeak.


00:37:25.986 --> 00:37:31.450
No wonder Congress' approval rating
is currently at 11 percent.


00:37:33.118 --> 00:37:36.246
Now, I'm sure a lot of you
have heard about a certain senator


00:37:36.330 --> 00:37:42.419
who it was revealed has been involved
in some... very unsavory business.


00:37:43.462 --> 00:37:47.841
What are we to learn from this?
We need accountability.


00:37:47.924 --> 00:37:50.010
We need openness.


00:37:50.093 --> 00:37:53.680
We need to know
what the people who represent us


00:37:53.764 --> 00:37:57.392
are doing with their time on our dime.


00:37:59.603 --> 00:38:01.313
To serve us better.


00:38:01.396 --> 00:38:02.981
Well, I'm happy to report


00:38:03.065 --> 00:38:08.278
that there's a woman
who is taking all of this very seriously


00:38:08.362 --> 00:38:13.367
and is taking some pretty dramatic steps
to address the problem.


00:38:13.450 --> 00:38:16.370
So please join me in welcoming


00:38:16.453 --> 00:38:20.123
Olivia Santos,
Congresswoman for District 14.


00:38:24.586 --> 00:38:25.706
That's right, Tom.


00:38:26.671 --> 00:38:30.675
I am as concerned as you are
about the need for citizens to know


00:38:30.759 --> 00:38:33.762
what their elected leaders are doing.


00:38:33.845 --> 00:38:39.893
So I intend to show exactly
how democracy can and should be.


00:38:44.856 --> 00:38:48.235
Starting today, my every meeting,


00:38:48.318 --> 00:38:53.031
my every phone call
and e-mail will be accessible


00:38:53.115 --> 00:38:56.785
to my constituents and to the world.


00:39:00.122 --> 00:39:02.499
In real time!


00:39:02.582 --> 00:39:08.213
It will all be on my True You page.
That's right. Thank you, thank you.


00:39:08.296 --> 00:39:11.550
Goodbye, backroom deals.
Goodbye, shady lobbyists.


00:39:11.633 --> 00:39:16.138
Hello, democracy. Open and accountable!


00:39:16.221 --> 00:39:17.806
That's right. That's a fact!


00:39:19.516 --> 00:39:22.853
- Where do you work?
- I work in customer experience.


00:39:22.936 --> 00:39:26.148
I started in CE, too. A lot of us did.


00:39:26.231 --> 00:39:28.150
Cool. Where do you work now?


00:39:28.233 --> 00:39:31.069
Biometrics, iris scanning,
facial recognition.


00:39:31.153 --> 00:39:33.822
Right now, I'm working on Child Track.


00:39:33.905 --> 00:39:36.825
The program protects children
from predators.


00:39:36.908 --> 00:39:39.786
The second a kid isn't where
they're supposed to be, an alert goes off


00:39:39.870 --> 00:39:42.372
and the kid can be tracked down
within 90 seconds.


00:39:42.456 --> 00:39:46.668
- Wow. Do they wear a bracelet or...?
- We place chips in the bone.


00:39:51.006 --> 00:39:52.007
Uh, you're...


00:39:54.509 --> 00:39:55.927
You're serious?


00:39:56.011 --> 00:40:00.182
I'm serious about immediately reducing
kidnapping, rape, and murder


00:40:00.265 --> 00:40:01.766
by 99 percent.


00:40:05.187 --> 00:40:06.456
I think I'm going to get a drink.


00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:07.939
- But...
- Oh.


00:40:08.023 --> 00:40:10.484
- It was nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.


00:40:16.907 --> 00:40:18.700
- Hey.
- Hey, check your e-mail


00:40:18.783 --> 00:40:22.245
- 'cause I sent you an invite.
- Awesome. Thank you. All right.


00:40:22.329 --> 00:40:24.372
- Yeah, good to see you.
- You too.


00:40:24.456 --> 00:40:26.124
- Hi, Mae! How's it going?
- Hey.


00:40:26.208 --> 00:40:27.792
- You having fun?
- Yeah, you?


00:40:27.876 --> 00:40:28.877
Yeah.


00:41:01.785 --> 00:41:03.620
- Hey.
- Hey.


00:41:08.041 --> 00:41:10.544
So, what did you think
about the whole presentation?


00:41:10.627 --> 00:41:12.254
- The Santos thing?
- Yeah.


00:41:14.256 --> 00:41:17.384
It's exciting. It's a...


00:41:17.467 --> 00:41:21.179
- Momentous moment in the history of...
- Okay, what did you really think?


00:41:26.393 --> 00:41:28.520
I thought it was a bit much.


00:41:28.603 --> 00:41:30.123
Just a little bit.


00:41:32.190 --> 00:41:33.692
So where does all the data go?


00:41:33.775 --> 00:41:37.112
Is it stored somewhere? In the Cloud?


00:41:38.154 --> 00:41:39.614
There's a physical place, too.


00:41:43.285 --> 00:41:44.578
Do you wanna see something?


00:42:06.766 --> 00:42:10.687
So you're gonna have to leave
your stuff outside before you go in.


00:42:10.770 --> 00:42:13.148
Just because you're not
supposed to... you know.


00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:23.575
So I'm just going to have to take... this.
And this.


00:42:36.254 --> 00:42:37.398
I want to thank you already for this.


00:42:37.422 --> 00:42:40.508
I love following strangers
down dark corridors.


00:42:40.592 --> 00:42:43.887
- This is the best.
- Almost there.


00:42:50.185 --> 00:42:54.814
This holds Congresswoman Santos,
every second of her from now on.


00:42:56.399 --> 00:42:58.234
And this will be the next one.


00:43:00.111 --> 00:43:03.031
- Just these?
- Oh, no. There's more.


00:43:06.743 --> 00:43:07.869
What is this?


00:43:07.952 --> 00:43:11.206
Well, it was supposed to be a part
of a subway but they abandoned it.


00:43:12.582 --> 00:43:15.043
- Where does it go?
- It connects to the bay.


00:43:15.126 --> 00:43:19.214
But I've traveled about a half a mile
into it, gets too wet.


00:43:23.134 --> 00:43:25.095
And that's where the rest of it will be.


00:43:26.388 --> 00:43:29.140
- The rest of what?
- The rest of it all.


00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:32.686
Santos is just the start.


00:43:33.812 --> 00:43:37.065
The plan is everything recorded,
seen, broadcast,


00:43:37.148 --> 00:43:41.152
all of it stored, analyzed,
available to the Circle in any way.


00:43:42.779 --> 00:43:44.179
You did your health intake, right?


00:43:45.407 --> 00:43:46.991
- Yeah.
- See, right? Look.


00:43:47.075 --> 00:43:50.453
Every step, every breath,
all of it stored.


00:43:52.122 --> 00:43:53.832
They can use it however they see fit.


00:43:53.915 --> 00:43:55.458
We're being studied.


00:43:55.542 --> 00:43:59.087
Everyone's data in the Circle
is being studied, monetized.


00:43:59.170 --> 00:44:00.630
It is voluntary.


00:44:01.798 --> 00:44:05.093
And isn't that the whole point?
Having everything in one place?


00:44:06.761 --> 00:44:08.847
I mean, that's True You.


00:44:08.930 --> 00:44:12.559
That's not why I invented True You.
Not even close.


00:44:12.642 --> 00:44:14.394
Ty Lafitte invented True You.


00:44:15.937 --> 00:44:17.564
Yeah.


00:44:17.647 --> 00:44:18.647
I'm Ty.


00:44:20.650 --> 00:44:21.860
No, you're not.


00:44:21.943 --> 00:44:24.612
Ty Lafitte has been off the grid
for years.


00:44:27.240 --> 00:44:30.243
Oh, my God.


00:44:30.326 --> 00:44:31.326
I'm Ty.


00:44:32.162 --> 00:44:34.080
Why the low profile?


00:44:34.164 --> 00:44:36.958
I just wanted to move through
this world like a normal person.


00:44:37.041 --> 00:44:39.544
I didn't wanna be watched every second.


00:44:39.627 --> 00:44:41.796
But I do spend a lot of time here.


00:44:41.880 --> 00:44:45.008
It's easier. I don't have to deal
with Stenton and Bailey.


00:44:45.091 --> 00:44:49.345
Not that I'm privy to their conversations
in their little sanctum.


00:44:49.429 --> 00:44:53.808
Holy shit. I'm in a cave with Ty Lafitte.


00:44:53.892 --> 00:45:00.023
Wait, hold on. So when we met,
you didn't know who I was?


00:45:00.106 --> 00:45:02.859
You never told me your name.


00:45:02.942 --> 00:45:04.986
And after a while,
it was just weird to ask.


00:45:06.780 --> 00:45:11.117
I knew I could trust you when I met you.
I don't know, I just thought to myself,


00:45:11.201 --> 00:45:13.401
this girl, she doesn't have
a cynical bone in her body.


00:45:14.370 --> 00:45:16.080
Reminds me of how I used to be.


00:45:17.081 --> 00:45:19.751
Listen, you can't tell anyone
that you were here.


00:45:19.834 --> 00:45:21.074
Okay? That I brought you here.


00:45:22.086 --> 00:45:23.087
Okay.


00:45:23.171 --> 00:45:27.509
Things at the Circle,
they need to change.


00:45:27.592 --> 00:45:29.802
Like what? Connecting the world?


00:45:31.095 --> 00:45:32.931
Insuring my parents?


00:45:33.014 --> 00:45:35.600
You should be really proud
of what you created.


00:45:35.683 --> 00:45:37.685
Look, proud? Proud of what?


00:45:37.769 --> 00:45:40.980
The disregard for privacy,
the use of personal data


00:45:41.064 --> 00:45:43.525
for the accumulation of wealth
and control?


00:45:43.608 --> 00:45:45.527
I'm not proud.
This is not what I created.


00:45:45.610 --> 00:45:47.588
Do you think Senator Williamson
being investigated


00:45:47.612 --> 00:45:49.697
by the FBI is just a coincidence?


00:45:49.781 --> 00:45:51.675
We can't be seen together,
and you have to promise me.


00:45:51.699 --> 00:45:53.499
You can't tell anyone
that we were down here.


00:46:07.757 --> 00:46:08.841
Hello?


00:46:08.925 --> 00:46:11.094
<i>Mae, you have a visitor here
to see you.</i>


00:46:11.177 --> 00:46:12.345
In the lobby?


00:46:18.434 --> 00:46:20.186
- Hey.
- Hey.


00:46:20.270 --> 00:46:23.314
- Are you okay?
- No, I'm really not okay.


00:46:24.399 --> 00:46:26.568
Why didn't you call, send a text?


00:46:26.651 --> 00:46:30.113
Because I wanted to see you in person.
I wanted to look you in the eyes.


00:46:31.656 --> 00:46:32.740
Okay.


00:46:32.824 --> 00:46:38.580
- Um, I'll sign you in.
- No, just wait, I...


00:46:38.663 --> 00:46:42.333
- The stuff you posted about my work.
- Your chandeliers?


00:46:42.417 --> 00:46:45.587
Yeah.
I know you meant well, but...


00:46:45.670 --> 00:46:48.089
What's the problem?
I thought I saw good stuff going up.


00:46:48.172 --> 00:46:50.091
They started calling me a deer killer.


00:46:51.092 --> 00:46:53.845
And then there's a campaign
to shut me down.


00:46:53.928 --> 00:46:56.347
I wasn't even online. I was working.


00:46:56.431 --> 00:46:59.225
And when I checked my e-mail,
there were death threats.


00:47:03.313 --> 00:47:04.355
I'm sorry.


00:47:04.439 --> 00:47:05.773
Death threats, Mae.


00:47:05.857 --> 00:47:07.692
Obviously, I never meant
for this to happen.


00:47:07.775 --> 00:47:10.069
But I... I can fix it.


00:47:14.449 --> 00:47:16.159
Mae.


00:47:16.242 --> 00:47:17.242
Mae.


00:47:18.578 --> 00:47:19.662
Will you listen to me?


00:47:20.955 --> 00:47:21.831
I can't do this.


00:47:21.914 --> 00:47:25.376
I can't be a part of this world
that you're creating.


00:47:25.460 --> 00:47:26.586
What are you talking about?


00:47:26.669 --> 00:47:28.755
Jesus Christ, look at these people!


00:47:28.838 --> 00:47:30.465
Does this really seem okay to you?


00:47:30.548 --> 00:47:32.276
Why don't you go
kill some more deer, Mercer?


00:47:32.300 --> 00:47:35.303
I never killed any deer.
I don't kill deer.


00:47:35.386 --> 00:47:38.848
Mercer, look, let's not do this here.


00:47:38.931 --> 00:47:40.475
Let's go upstairs.


00:47:40.558 --> 00:47:42.935
- I'll figure it out.
- Do you ever leave this place, Mae?


00:47:44.520 --> 00:47:48.274
We used to go on adventures
and have fun and see things,


00:47:48.358 --> 00:47:50.818
and you were brave and excited.


00:47:52.654 --> 00:47:54.947
And now it's all filtered through this.


00:48:01.412 --> 00:48:02.622
Bye.


00:51:09.100 --> 00:51:10.601
I can't believe I was so stupid.


00:51:12.603 --> 00:51:14.289
I'm so lucky that you guys
were around here.


00:51:14.313 --> 00:51:15.982
Luck? Hell.


00:51:16.065 --> 00:51:18.484
SeeChange cameras saved your life.


00:51:18.568 --> 00:51:20.027
A cop saw you go through the fence,


00:51:20.111 --> 00:51:22.488
and then we got a call
from someone watching the seals.


00:51:28.953 --> 00:51:30.538
So, Mae, how do you feel?


00:51:34.500 --> 00:51:35.500
Ashamed.


00:51:35.543 --> 00:51:38.254
I... I barely slept.


00:51:38.337 --> 00:51:40.965
Well, I'm just glad you're safe.
We all are.


00:51:42.008 --> 00:51:44.510
And everyone
who watched your adventure online.


00:51:44.594 --> 00:51:47.013
I hope your folks weren't too worried.


00:51:47.096 --> 00:51:48.139
They're doing okay.


00:51:49.724 --> 00:51:51.851
I understand
your father's condition has improved.


00:51:53.185 --> 00:51:54.437
Very much.


00:51:58.774 --> 00:51:59.775
Mr. Bailey...


00:52:00.776 --> 00:52:03.279
I want to say how much
I value my position here...


00:52:05.281 --> 00:52:06.949
and what you've done for my family.


00:52:07.033 --> 00:52:10.620
Mae, you're a valued part of the Circle,
and you're family.


00:52:10.703 --> 00:52:13.164
And we care about
everybody you care about.


00:52:14.332 --> 00:52:17.919
You know, this incident
has actually shown a lot of people


00:52:18.002 --> 00:52:19.211
the necessity for SeeChange.


00:52:20.755 --> 00:52:23.132
I'm so glad those cameras were there.


00:52:23.215 --> 00:52:24.693
I wish you would've known
there was a camera there


00:52:24.717 --> 00:52:26.969
before you swiped that kayak,
but...


00:52:29.597 --> 00:52:32.892
Mae, Tom and I want to talk you
about an idea, but before we do,


00:52:34.936 --> 00:52:36.646
is there anything you want to tell us?


00:52:41.317 --> 00:52:43.527
That I've been here before?


00:52:43.611 --> 00:52:46.697
It's only our lies that get us in trouble,
the things we hide.


00:52:48.282 --> 00:52:50.701
Of course I know you've
been in here before.


00:52:52.703 --> 00:52:55.456
And now that I know your secret,
do you feel better or worse?


00:53:00.169 --> 00:53:02.046
Better.


00:53:02.129 --> 00:53:03.255
Relieved, actually.


00:53:05.091 --> 00:53:08.552
I am a believer
in the perfectibility of human beings.


00:53:08.636 --> 00:53:12.264
When we are our best selves,
the possibilities are endless.


00:53:12.348 --> 00:53:14.392
There isn't a problem
that we cannot solve.


00:53:14.475 --> 00:53:18.229
We can cure any disease
and we can end hunger, and...


00:53:19.939 --> 00:53:24.527
Without secrets, without the hoarding
of knowledge and information,


00:53:24.610 --> 00:53:27.405
we can finally realize our potential.


00:53:30.449 --> 00:53:32.702
This is a special Dream Friday.


00:53:32.785 --> 00:53:37.999
I thought I'd change it up a bit today
and not have a speech but an interview.


00:53:39.125 --> 00:53:41.502
I had a conversation
with a young Circler the other day


00:53:41.585 --> 00:53:43.087
that I wanted to share with you.


00:53:44.088 --> 00:53:47.425
I've asked Mae Holland,
who some of you might know


00:53:47.508 --> 00:53:51.012
as one of our new guppies in customer
experience, to join me today.


00:53:51.095 --> 00:53:55.266
Mae's only been with us for a few months
but she has made quite an impression.


00:53:55.349 --> 00:53:57.685
Let's bring her out. Mae Holland!


00:54:08.154 --> 00:54:10.031
Well, hi, Mae, how are you?


00:54:10.114 --> 00:54:11.115
- Terrified.
- Mm.


00:54:11.198 --> 00:54:12.658
Don't be nervous.


00:54:12.742 --> 00:54:14.702
Easy for you to say.


00:54:15.995 --> 00:54:17.621
All right, Mae, we have all seen


00:54:17.705 --> 00:54:19.425
the SeeChange footage
from the other night,


00:54:19.498 --> 00:54:21.709
but please describe
what happened for us.


00:54:23.377 --> 00:54:24.712
I committed a crime.


00:54:25.963 --> 00:54:29.467
I borrowed a kayak
without the owner's knowledge,


00:54:29.550 --> 00:54:31.070
paddled out to the middle of the bay,


00:54:32.428 --> 00:54:35.097
and I wasn't wearing a life jacket.


00:54:35.181 --> 00:54:39.518
Well, I'm not sure
that's exactly a capital offense,


00:54:39.602 --> 00:54:41.729
but you did risk your own life.


00:54:42.980 --> 00:54:45.316
So, Mae, do you think
you behave better or worse


00:54:45.399 --> 00:54:48.235
- when you are being watched?
- Better.


00:54:49.820 --> 00:54:51.363
Without a doubt.


00:54:51.447 --> 00:54:54.200
And what happens when you're alone
and unobserved?


00:54:55.284 --> 00:54:57.161
Well, for starters, I steal kayaks.


00:54:58.746 --> 00:55:00.998
Jesus. She's a natural.


00:55:01.082 --> 00:55:05.211
Seriously. I do things I don't wanna do.


00:55:06.504 --> 00:55:07.838
I lie.


00:55:07.922 --> 00:55:09.858
When we spoke the other day,
you had a way of putting it


00:55:09.882 --> 00:55:11.526
that I thought
was very interesting and succinct.


00:55:11.550 --> 00:55:13.260
Could you tell us all what you said?


00:55:15.137 --> 00:55:17.264
I said that secrets are lies.


00:55:18.474 --> 00:55:19.850
Secrets are lies.


00:55:21.811 --> 00:55:22.811
Go on.


00:55:23.979 --> 00:55:29.235
Secrets are what make crimes possible.


00:55:29.318 --> 00:55:32.113
We behave worse
when we're not accountable.


00:55:33.531 --> 00:55:35.908
I was my worst self


00:55:35.991 --> 00:55:38.577
because I didn't think
anyone was watching.


00:55:40.204 --> 00:55:41.204
I thought...


00:55:42.790 --> 00:55:43.791
that I was alone.


00:55:44.917 --> 00:55:48.671
So, what did you see
on this kayaking trip, Mae?


00:55:48.754 --> 00:55:50.965
- Was it beautiful?
- It was.


00:55:52.133 --> 00:55:53.843
There was almost a full moon.


00:55:54.969 --> 00:55:56.220
And it felt like...


00:55:58.264 --> 00:56:01.684
it felt like I was paddling
through liquid silver.


00:56:01.767 --> 00:56:04.270
Hmm, sounds incredible.


00:56:05.354 --> 00:56:07.439
But there's no record of any of it.


00:56:07.523 --> 00:56:09.525
- Not outside my own memory.
- Hmm.


00:56:12.611 --> 00:56:13.988
Okay.


00:56:14.071 --> 00:56:16.532
So now here we get
into something personal.


00:56:16.615 --> 00:56:20.744
As you know, I have a son, Gunner,


00:56:21.829 --> 00:56:24.206
who was born with cerebral palsy.


00:56:24.290 --> 00:56:26.375
And though he's living a very full life


00:56:26.458 --> 00:56:29.420
and we're always trying
to improve his opportunities,


00:56:29.503 --> 00:56:32.548
he is confined to a wheelchair.


00:56:32.631 --> 00:56:35.634
He can't walk.


00:56:35.718 --> 00:56:37.636
He can't run.


00:56:37.720 --> 00:56:39.889
He can't go kayaking.


00:56:41.098 --> 00:56:44.643
So, what does he do if he wants
to experience something like that?


00:56:44.727 --> 00:56:46.729
Well, he watches video.


00:56:46.812 --> 00:56:48.272
He looks at pictures.


00:56:49.481 --> 00:56:53.611
And much of his experiences of the world
come through the experience of others.


00:56:53.694 --> 00:56:57.323
When he experiences the view
of a Circler climbing Mount Kenya,


00:56:57.406 --> 00:56:59.533
he feels like he's climbed Mount Kenya.


00:57:01.744 --> 00:57:05.206
When he sees firsthand video
from an America's Cup crew member,


00:57:05.289 --> 00:57:10.377
he feels in some way that he's sailed
in the America's Cup too.


00:57:10.461 --> 00:57:13.839
And these experiences
were facilitated by generous humans


00:57:13.923 --> 00:57:17.009
who shared what they saw with the world,
my son included,


00:57:17.092 --> 00:57:19.571
and there are many others out there
that are just like Gunner.


00:57:19.595 --> 00:57:22.223
Now does it feel right
to have deprived them


00:57:22.306 --> 00:57:24.016
of seeing what you saw, Mae?


00:57:27.686 --> 00:57:28.687
It doesn't.


00:57:31.857 --> 00:57:33.025
It feels very wrong.


00:57:35.986 --> 00:57:37.112
It was selfish.


00:57:38.989 --> 00:57:44.328
When you deprive others of experiences
like the ones I had,


00:57:47.206 --> 00:57:49.208
you're essentially stealing from them.


00:57:50.251 --> 00:57:54.672
Knowledge is a basic human right.


00:57:57.132 --> 00:58:03.514
Access to all possible human experience
is a basic human right.


00:58:16.902 --> 00:58:20.447
Thank you, Mae, for your candor
and your consummate humanity.


00:58:20.531 --> 00:58:22.616
Now... now the best part.


00:58:22.700 --> 00:58:25.327
In the interest of sharing
everything she can with the world,


00:58:25.411 --> 00:58:28.122
Mae has, uh... Mae has some news.


00:58:31.750 --> 00:58:32.918
From now on...


00:58:34.837 --> 00:58:41.552
I'll be wearing a modified
SeeChange camera at all times.


00:58:44.054 --> 00:58:46.265
I'm going fully transparent.


00:58:49.268 --> 00:58:51.312
Well, maybe not in the bathroom.


00:58:53.355 --> 00:58:56.859
I'll be starting immediately.


00:58:56.942 --> 00:58:57.942
That's right.


00:58:59.737 --> 00:59:03.324
Mae will be the first Circler
to share every single second


00:59:03.407 --> 00:59:06.535
of her work
and her personal life with the public,


00:59:06.619 --> 00:59:08.912
so let's give her an even bigger hand!


00:59:18.964 --> 00:59:20.215
I'm proud of you.


00:59:25.846 --> 00:59:28.641
Mae! Mae! Mae! Mae!


00:59:28.724 --> 00:59:30.017
Mae! Mae! Mae! Mae!


00:59:48.661 --> 00:59:50.013
<i>Hello.</i>


00:59:50.037 --> 00:59:51.997
Good morning, everyone.


00:59:52.081 --> 00:59:55.292
<i>The sun is shining,
and I definitely need my coffee.</i>


00:59:56.502 --> 00:59:59.463
We are in my third week of transparency,


00:59:59.546 --> 01:00:01.632
and we're up to...


01:00:01.715 --> 01:00:05.803
2,308,007 viewers.


01:00:05.886 --> 01:00:06.720
<i>Wow.</i>


01:00:06.804 --> 01:00:08.972
Did I tell you guys
I'm not a morning person?


01:00:10.140 --> 01:00:13.852
Okay, among all of those viewers...


01:00:14.978 --> 01:00:15.978
are...


01:00:16.814 --> 01:00:19.191
- <i>I don't even know what...</i>
- Hi, Mom, hi, Dad.


01:00:19.274 --> 01:00:21.110
<i>- Hi!
- Hi, Mae.</i>


01:00:21.193 --> 01:00:22.695
Say hello to everyone.


01:00:22.778 --> 01:00:24.822
<i>Hello, Circle.</i>


01:00:24.905 --> 01:00:27.408
Look. We match.


01:00:27.491 --> 01:00:29.076
<i>- Yeah.
- We sure do.</i>


01:00:29.159 --> 01:00:31.620
<i>- These are great.
- Very stylish.</i>


01:00:31.704 --> 01:00:33.872
I love you guys. I'll see you later.


01:00:33.956 --> 01:00:36.250
<i>- Love you. Bye.
- Love you too, sweetheart.</i>


01:00:41.672 --> 01:00:45.467
- Hello, Circlers.
<i>- Hi, Mae.</i>


01:00:45.551 --> 01:00:48.554
So this is where I worked
when I first arrived at the Circle,


01:00:48.637 --> 01:00:52.015
and this is where I still work
at the Circle.


01:00:52.099 --> 01:00:54.810
Because even though I have you guys
looking over my shoulder...


01:00:54.893 --> 01:00:58.188
I'm still basically a guppy.


01:00:58.272 --> 01:01:03.026
- Go! Do it!
- Do it! Do it! Do it!


01:01:03.110 --> 01:01:05.988
<i>Oh, my God!</i>


01:01:06.071 --> 01:01:08.258
<i>What are you doing?
Tell your own stories, sheep.</i>


01:01:08.282 --> 01:01:10.659
<i>Stop looking at us, sheep!</i>


01:01:10.743 --> 01:01:12.369
<i>All right, that's our scene.</i>


01:01:12.453 --> 01:01:15.205
Whoo!


01:01:15.289 --> 01:01:19.710
Sorry about that. Okay, so, next up,
we just need the name of a person


01:01:19.793 --> 01:01:23.630
who is historically significant.


01:01:23.714 --> 01:01:26.800
- Mae Holland!
- Oh, my God!


01:01:26.884 --> 01:01:28.385
<i>Sorry, we don't know who that is.</i>


01:01:28.469 --> 01:01:31.847
She's nobody, trust me.


01:01:34.099 --> 01:01:35.768
Home sweet home.


01:01:35.851 --> 01:01:38.103
Speaking of which,
let's check in with the folks.


01:01:40.439 --> 01:01:42.691
Hey, where are you guys?


01:01:47.571 --> 01:01:48.572
<i>Yeah.</i>


01:01:48.655 --> 01:01:51.575
<i>- Yeah. There you go.</i>
- Whoa! Ah!


01:01:51.658 --> 01:01:53.035
Um...


01:01:53.118 --> 01:01:55.329
Uh, I hope the engineers are watching,


01:01:55.412 --> 01:01:57.724
because that definitely
shouldn't have happened


01:01:57.748 --> 01:02:01.084
and, um, it definitely
won't happen again.


01:02:01.168 --> 01:02:02.854
You can trust me there.


01:02:13.388 --> 01:02:15.766
<i>This is me brushing my teeth.</i>


01:02:15.849 --> 01:02:17.935
<i>If you're enjoying this,
I'm worried about you.</i>


01:02:34.409 --> 01:02:35.869
Good night, everyone.


01:02:37.079 --> 01:02:39.623
I'm not gonna lie, it was a crazy day,


01:02:39.706 --> 01:02:42.793
but I'm happy I had you with me,
each and every one of you.


01:03:32.843 --> 01:03:34.344
<i>Never a boring day here.</i>


01:03:35.512 --> 01:03:38.640
A lot of you guys have been asking me
about this sculpture,


01:03:38.724 --> 01:03:40.017
which is a treat for me.


01:03:40.100 --> 01:03:42.811
I finally get to use
my art history degree.


01:03:42.895 --> 01:03:45.772
<i>This was made by an artist
who has frequently been in trouble</i>


01:03:45.856 --> 01:03:47.357
<i>in his own country.</i>


01:03:47.441 --> 01:03:51.445
I wanna thank all the watchers
who sent frowns to the government there,


01:03:51.528 --> 01:03:54.489
both for their persecution of the artist


01:03:54.573 --> 01:03:57.242
and for their restriction
on Internet freedoms.


01:03:57.326 --> 01:04:01.705
We have sent 180 million frowns
from the U.S. alone.


01:04:01.788 --> 01:04:04.917
You can bet that's gonna have
an effect on the regime.


01:04:07.502 --> 01:04:08.712
Annie.


01:04:11.506 --> 01:04:12.507
<i>Annie.</i>


01:04:14.009 --> 01:04:15.010
Annie!


01:04:18.430 --> 01:04:19.430
Annie!


01:04:26.772 --> 01:04:27.814
Annie!


01:04:29.858 --> 01:04:32.527
Annie Allerton!


01:04:43.455 --> 01:04:44.455
Are we alone?


01:04:46.041 --> 01:04:49.336
I have three minutes to use the bathroom
without the video on.


01:04:49.419 --> 01:04:50.837
Pervs.


01:04:52.965 --> 01:04:54.132
How are you?


01:04:56.426 --> 01:04:58.136
- Good.
- Well...


01:04:58.220 --> 01:04:59.221
you should be good.


01:04:59.304 --> 01:05:00.305
You're killing it.


01:05:01.556 --> 01:05:02.391
Do you think?


01:05:02.474 --> 01:05:04.434
Oh, come on,
false modesty won't work here.


01:05:04.518 --> 01:05:05.518
You should be psyched.


01:05:06.728 --> 01:05:07.728
Yeah.


01:05:08.814 --> 01:05:10.857
- I am.
- You're a meteor. It's insane.


01:05:10.941 --> 01:05:12.961
People are coming to me
trying to get to you.


01:05:12.985 --> 01:05:14.152
It's... crazy.


01:05:15.988 --> 01:05:17.197
How are you really, Annie?


01:05:18.573 --> 01:05:19.658
You look wiped.


01:05:19.741 --> 01:05:22.744
Thanks, Mae.
You know how much I like to be told


01:05:22.828 --> 01:05:24.639
right after I appear
in front of your millions


01:05:24.663 --> 01:05:27.290
that I look terrible.
Thank you, you're sweet.


01:05:27.374 --> 01:05:28.667
Let me take you for dinner.


01:05:31.044 --> 01:05:32.587
You gotta eat sometime.


01:05:32.671 --> 01:05:36.133
Your camera and me looking so terrible.
That sounds fantastic, but, no.


01:05:36.216 --> 01:05:38.802
I just...
I'm worried about you.


01:05:38.885 --> 01:05:41.972
- You're working too hard.
- You're worried about me?


01:05:42.055 --> 01:05:43.807
You're... I'm worried about the EU,


01:05:43.890 --> 01:05:45.326
but it's good to know
you're worried about me.


01:05:45.350 --> 01:05:47.811
- I didn't mean it that way.
- Don't worry in any way.


01:05:51.189 --> 01:05:54.109
- I know you can handle it.
- Oh, thank you, Mae.


01:05:54.192 --> 01:05:56.778
Your confidence in me
will be the wind beneath my wings.


01:05:58.739 --> 01:05:59.739
How are your parents?


01:06:03.326 --> 01:06:04.327
Sorry.


01:06:06.788 --> 01:06:09.583
They're over that little... episode?


01:06:13.795 --> 01:06:16.465
I haven't heard from them, actually.


01:06:18.383 --> 01:06:20.510
I guess it'll just take some time.


01:06:20.594 --> 01:06:22.721
Sorry, Mae. Shit.


01:06:24.890 --> 01:06:25.891
I'm fried.


01:06:28.060 --> 01:06:29.770
And I have to pull another all-nighter.


01:06:31.354 --> 01:06:33.565
Stenton is on my ass all the time.


01:06:33.648 --> 01:06:37.110
I shouldn't have questioned him
about that whole Senator Williamson thing.


01:06:38.195 --> 01:06:41.823
And then...
there's all the new work on Completion.


01:06:42.908 --> 01:06:44.743
But I assume Bailey told you about that.


01:06:47.788 --> 01:06:49.498
Of course.


01:06:49.581 --> 01:06:50.874
Of course.


01:06:50.957 --> 01:06:53.117
I'll be there
at the Concept Kingdom meeting tomorrow.


01:06:53.168 --> 01:06:54.168
What?


01:06:56.171 --> 01:06:59.007
- There's a Concept Kingdom...
- I know what it is. But you're going?


01:06:59.091 --> 01:07:03.887
- Yeah. Bailey said I should be there.
- And broadcast it?


01:07:05.764 --> 01:07:07.307
- Is that a problem?
- No.


01:07:07.390 --> 01:07:10.102
I'm just surprised, that's all.
Those meetings are sensitive.


01:07:10.185 --> 01:07:13.271
You know, maybe he's planning on
having you attend just the beginning.


01:07:13.355 --> 01:07:15.607
I mean, Mae,
you're in over your head.


01:07:18.193 --> 01:07:19.736
Are you leaving?


01:07:19.820 --> 01:07:21.154
Yeah.


01:07:22.239 --> 01:07:24.825
I'm really so far behind on everything,
I could just die.


01:07:28.787 --> 01:07:29.788
Annie?


01:07:31.456 --> 01:07:32.896
<i>Hello.</i>


01:07:34.793 --> 01:07:35.793
See ya.


01:07:46.054 --> 01:07:50.475
<i>So here's how many people voted
in the last national election.</i>


01:07:50.559 --> 01:07:53.520
<i>Here's how many were eligible to vote.</i>


01:07:55.647 --> 01:07:59.860
And here is how many Americans
are registered in the Circle.


01:08:01.444 --> 01:08:03.280
And there's that guy.


01:08:03.363 --> 01:08:05.490
So now we get down to the meat
of today's session,


01:08:05.574 --> 01:08:08.702
something we've discussed
with Congresswoman Santos and others.


01:08:10.078 --> 01:08:16.501
What if... your Circle profile
automatically registered you to vote?


01:08:18.336 --> 01:08:20.672
With True You,
in order to set up a profile,


01:08:20.755 --> 01:08:23.425
you have to be a real person
with a real address,


01:08:23.508 --> 01:08:27.137
the complete personal info,
real social security number,


01:08:27.220 --> 01:08:29.347
with all the information
that the government wants


01:08:29.431 --> 01:08:31.391
when you register to vote.


01:08:31.474 --> 01:08:35.520
So why wouldn't the state
consider you already registered


01:08:35.604 --> 01:08:37.230
when you open a Circle account?


01:08:38.315 --> 01:08:39.900
There's no reason.


01:08:39.983 --> 01:08:44.946
Uh, we've got verbal commitments
from governors of almost every state,


01:08:45.030 --> 01:08:50.035
and they've agreed to push for legislation
that would make your Circle account


01:08:50.118 --> 01:08:52.662
an automatic path to voter registration.


01:08:54.915 --> 01:08:55.915
Bravo.


01:08:59.502 --> 01:09:00.962
I think it's a great idea.


01:09:06.301 --> 01:09:08.762
Well, yes, Mae.


01:09:12.432 --> 01:09:14.684
Why couldn't we take it one step further?


01:09:15.685 --> 01:09:17.062
I mean, I...


01:09:17.145 --> 01:09:20.732
I don't have it
completely figured out yet...


01:09:20.815 --> 01:09:23.944
No, no, no, go on, go on, go on.
You started really well there.


01:09:24.027 --> 01:09:26.988
I like those words, "one step further."
It's how the company was built.


01:09:29.157 --> 01:09:33.119
Okay. Working backwards from your goal...


01:09:33.203 --> 01:09:34.788
Go on.


01:09:34.871 --> 01:09:37.749
We all agree 100 percent participation
is what we'd like.


01:09:37.832 --> 01:09:40.335
A hundred percent participation
is the ideal.


01:09:40.418 --> 01:09:43.588
That's the idealist's ideal, yes.


01:09:43.672 --> 01:09:48.051
And we already have 83 percent
of voting-age Americans


01:09:48.134 --> 01:09:49.552
registered to the Circle.


01:09:49.636 --> 01:09:50.845
Yep.


01:09:50.929 --> 01:09:52.764
And it seems we're on our way


01:09:52.847 --> 01:09:57.060
to users being able to register it
and maybe actually vote


01:09:57.143 --> 01:09:58.204
through their Circle account.


01:09:58.228 --> 01:10:02.774
Well, that's a small leap, but... Yes.
Okay, go on.


01:10:04.901 --> 01:10:10.031
Why not require every voting-age citizen
to have a Circle account?


01:10:10.115 --> 01:10:12.951
Let her finish. Let her finish.


01:10:13.034 --> 01:10:16.413
Okay, I know the initial reaction
will be resistance.


01:10:16.496 --> 01:10:19.916
I mean, how can we require anyone
to use our services?


01:10:21.001 --> 01:10:22.937
But there are all kinds of things
that are mandatory


01:10:22.961 --> 01:10:24.105
for citizens of this country.


01:10:24.129 --> 01:10:26.089
Yep. We have to pay taxes
we don't wanna pay.


01:10:26.172 --> 01:10:27.799
And we have to pay for Social Security.


01:10:27.882 --> 01:10:30.552
- We have to serve on juries.
- Right.


01:10:30.635 --> 01:10:33.054
And we have to pee indoors,
not on the street.


01:10:33.138 --> 01:10:35.432
- We do. We do have to be pee indoors.
- She's right.


01:10:35.515 --> 01:10:37.726
I mean, we have thousands of laws.


01:10:37.809 --> 01:10:40.979
We require citizens
to do hundreds of things,


01:10:41.062 --> 01:10:43.773
but we don't require them to vote?


01:10:45.650 --> 01:10:49.070
They are already doing this
in dozens of countries.


01:10:49.154 --> 01:10:53.575
With the technology we already have,
users will already be registered.


01:10:53.658 --> 01:10:58.788
That means you have 241 million voters
eligible to vote.


01:10:58.872 --> 01:11:03.418
You have 241 million voters
that have to vote.


01:11:05.962 --> 01:11:07.881
You have the full will of the nation.


01:11:07.964 --> 01:11:10.633
- And how exactly do we do that?
- Annie.


01:11:11.885 --> 01:11:16.389
Well, you know,
it could be something as simple as a zing,


01:11:16.473 --> 01:11:20.810
"Hey, Annie.
Remember to take five minutes and vote."


01:11:20.894 --> 01:11:25.106
It's what we would do
with our own surveys. You know that.


01:11:25.190 --> 01:11:29.527
- But, Mae, what about stragglers?
- We all pay taxes.


01:11:31.154 --> 01:11:33.531
And what, 80 percent of us
did that online last year.


01:11:35.909 --> 01:11:38.703
Why not stop duplicating services?


01:11:39.871 --> 01:11:43.166
Why not just make it all part
of one unified system?


01:11:43.249 --> 01:11:48.129
You could pay your taxes,
vote, pay your parking ticket,


01:11:48.213 --> 01:11:50.423
all through your Circle account.


01:11:50.507 --> 01:11:53.218
We'd save users hundreds of hours
of inconvenience.


01:11:53.301 --> 01:11:56.554
- We'd save the government billions.
- Hundreds of billions.


01:11:56.638 --> 01:11:58.848
We could eliminate half of it overnight.


01:11:58.932 --> 01:12:01.213
Why wouldn't the government
just build a similar service?


01:12:01.267 --> 01:12:02.560
Why the hell does it need us?


01:12:05.605 --> 01:12:08.608
It would cost too much.
They don't have the expertise.


01:12:08.691 --> 01:12:12.070
- We already have the infrastructure.
- You're absolutely right.


01:12:12.153 --> 01:12:16.491
The government needs us
more than we need them.


01:12:16.574 --> 01:12:20.870
Imagine having the full will
of the people.


01:12:22.455 --> 01:12:23.498
Instantly.


01:12:25.750 --> 01:12:29.129
You'd have... true democracy


01:12:30.213 --> 01:12:32.465
for the first time in human history.


01:12:33.842 --> 01:12:35.682
This is such bullshit.


01:12:40.765 --> 01:12:43.268
No voter suppression.
You're voting from home.


01:12:43.351 --> 01:12:46.020
<i>Think of the implications
for totalitarian regimes.</i>


01:12:46.104 --> 01:12:47.689
No more rigged elections.


01:12:47.772 --> 01:12:49.875
The UN can demand
that they're held through the Circle.


01:12:49.899 --> 01:12:52.902
But only if everyone is heard.


01:12:52.986 --> 01:12:55.738
Every human on Earth.


01:12:55.822 --> 01:12:57.824
We can take the pulse of the nation,


01:12:57.907 --> 01:13:01.411
<i>everyone in the world in seconds
on any subject.</i>


01:13:01.494 --> 01:13:03.663
- It's the only way, right?
- Right.


01:13:03.746 --> 01:13:04.998
Yeah!


01:13:07.750 --> 01:13:11.838
So it looks like I've got 20 minutes here.


01:13:11.921 --> 01:13:14.382
Let's try Mom and Dad.


01:13:17.218 --> 01:13:18.779
Hey, Mae, what's up?


01:13:18.803 --> 01:13:19.804
Hi.


01:13:25.768 --> 01:13:28.271
- Hey, where have you been?
<i>- Mae.</i>


01:13:28.354 --> 01:13:31.524
I've seen your cameras aren't working.
I can send Tech out...


01:13:31.608 --> 01:13:34.152
<i>Yeah, we disconnected them.</i>


01:13:34.235 --> 01:13:35.778
<i>Sweetheart...</i>


01:13:36.946 --> 01:13:40.325
<i>We're sorry, but we,
we can't keep doing this.</i>


01:13:40.408 --> 01:13:44.120
<i>It's... Well, it's more than we ever...</i>


01:13:44.204 --> 01:13:47.290
<i>You know we love you,
and we don't wanna...</i>


01:13:47.373 --> 01:13:51.628
<i>- We love you. We love you.
- We have to have our privacy.</i>


01:13:51.711 --> 01:13:55.215
<i>And we're worried you don't realize
what you've gotten yourself into.</i>


01:13:55.298 --> 01:13:56.341
I'm so sorry.


01:13:57.467 --> 01:13:58.760
I didn't mean to hurt you.


01:13:58.843 --> 01:14:00.779
<i>- It's not you. It's us.
- No, we know that.</i>


01:14:00.803 --> 01:14:03.765
<i>- Yeah, it's us.
- We just need you to understand that.</i>


01:14:06.601 --> 01:14:09.729
- I love you.
<i>- We love you too.</i>


01:14:09.812 --> 01:14:10.812
<i>Bye, honey.</i>


01:14:14.108 --> 01:14:16.869
<i>- </i>You're the one, Mae!
- You're changing the world, Mae!


01:14:34.921 --> 01:14:39.634
Hello, everyone. I'm Mae Holland.


01:14:43.096 --> 01:14:46.224
Okay.


01:14:46.307 --> 01:14:49.936
We've had some good weeks
thinking about Completion,


01:14:50.019 --> 01:14:53.189
envisioning a day when everyone
is in the Circle,


01:14:53.273 --> 01:14:57.235
when all of humanity
is connected and united.


01:14:58.319 --> 01:14:59.988
And it will happen.


01:15:00.071 --> 01:15:02.532
Already, 22 nations have agreed
in principle


01:15:02.615 --> 01:15:05.868
to operate their elections
through the Circle


01:15:05.952 --> 01:15:10.790
with mandatory participation
from all of its citizens.


01:15:16.754 --> 01:15:22.302
And when everyone is united,
everyone is known, heard, accountable,


01:15:23.553 --> 01:15:25.221
they can be better served.


01:15:25.305 --> 01:15:29.767
But what about the people
who aren't part of the Circle,


01:15:29.851 --> 01:15:33.271
who don't want to be woven
into the fabric of society


01:15:33.354 --> 01:15:36.983
or worse, want to tear out that fabric?


01:15:37.984 --> 01:15:42.196
I want to introduce Soul Search.


01:15:42.280 --> 01:15:46.242
And we're putting it to the test
in front of the entire Circle audience,


01:15:46.326 --> 01:15:49.621
here and globally.


01:15:49.704 --> 01:15:50.705
Do you feel ready?


01:15:50.788 --> 01:15:52.832
Hell, yeah!


01:15:52.915 --> 01:15:54.500
<i>You went well so far.</i>


01:15:54.584 --> 01:15:55.668
<i>Great.</i>


01:15:58.212 --> 01:16:03.301
In seconds, the computer will select,
at random, a fugitive from justice,


01:16:03.384 --> 01:16:06.804
a proven menace to our global community.


01:16:07.972 --> 01:16:10.975
Our assertion is that
whoever he or she is,


01:16:11.059 --> 01:16:15.396
Soul Search will locate them
in under 20 minutes.


01:16:15.480 --> 01:16:17.357
Wow.


01:16:20.735 --> 01:16:22.362
Let's select that fugitive.


01:16:24.113 --> 01:16:25.698
Okay.


01:16:25.782 --> 01:16:28.368
This is Fiona Highbridge,


01:16:28.451 --> 01:16:31.371
44 years old born in Manchester, England.


01:16:31.454 --> 01:16:34.749
She was convicted
of triple murder in 2004.


01:16:34.832 --> 01:16:37.251
She locked her three children
in the closet


01:16:38.294 --> 01:16:40.588
and went on holiday to Spain.


01:16:42.882 --> 01:16:44.133
They all starved.


01:16:45.927 --> 01:16:48.179
She was sent to prison in England
but escaped


01:16:48.262 --> 01:16:51.307
with the help of a guard
who she apparently seduced.


01:16:52.975 --> 01:16:57.313
Over a billion people are watching.


01:16:57.397 --> 01:16:59.273
Let's see how many are in the UK.


01:17:00.858 --> 01:17:03.569
Do you all think 14 million Brits


01:17:03.653 --> 01:17:06.364
and a billion global
participants worldwide


01:17:06.447 --> 01:17:09.659
can find Fiona Highbridge
in under 20 minutes?


01:17:09.742 --> 01:17:11.062
Yeah!


01:17:12.453 --> 01:17:14.372
Yeah!


01:17:14.455 --> 01:17:16.499
All right.


01:17:16.582 --> 01:17:18.167
Let's bring up the clock.


01:17:19.168 --> 01:17:20.878
Good.


01:17:20.962 --> 01:17:24.340
Is everyone ready?


01:17:24.424 --> 01:17:25.800
On your mark...


01:17:27.468 --> 01:17:28.553
get set...


01:17:29.887 --> 01:17:30.887
go!


01:17:38.730 --> 01:17:40.898
Okay, time to get serious.


01:18:10.344 --> 01:18:11.429
<i>I've got her, Mae.</i>


01:18:12.972 --> 01:18:13.972
<i>That's her.</i>


01:18:14.891 --> 01:18:16.851
Be careful.


01:18:16.934 --> 01:18:17.934
<i>I will.</i>


01:18:24.984 --> 01:18:28.196
Whoa!


01:18:29.363 --> 01:18:30.363
Whoa!


01:18:39.165 --> 01:18:40.750
<i>Fiona! Stop!</i>


01:18:41.834 --> 01:18:44.879
- Follow her.
<i>- Stop her!</i>


01:18:44.962 --> 01:18:46.589
<i>Somebody stop her! She's a killer!</i>


01:18:47.882 --> 01:18:48.883
<i>Stop her!</i>


01:18:49.884 --> 01:18:51.177
<i>Somebody stop her!</i>


01:18:52.678 --> 01:18:54.388
- Go!
- Grab her!


01:18:57.517 --> 01:18:59.393
<i>Stop her!</i>


01:18:59.477 --> 01:19:01.604
<i>Somebody grab her!</i>


01:19:01.687 --> 01:19:04.190
- <i>She must be kept safe.</i>
- Keep her safe.


01:19:04.273 --> 01:19:07.527
<i>- It's Fiona Highbridge!</i>
<i>- </i><i>No! No! No!</i>


01:19:19.789 --> 01:19:20.873
<i>All right. Watch it.</i>


01:19:23.543 --> 01:19:24.543
<i>No.</i>


01:19:26.170 --> 01:19:27.170
Stop the clock.


01:19:29.757 --> 01:19:31.050
Soul Search, baby!


01:19:31.133 --> 01:19:33.594
I guess that's it.


01:19:43.312 --> 01:19:45.731
Let's do another!


01:19:45.815 --> 01:19:46.816
We could.


01:19:48.150 --> 01:19:49.962
- Um...
<i>- Maybe not a fugitive this time.</i>


01:19:49.986 --> 01:19:51.946
Let's try for a regular civilian.


01:19:53.406 --> 01:19:55.241
Okay, everyone.


01:19:55.324 --> 01:19:58.119
We're gonna find a regular person.


01:19:59.120 --> 01:20:01.247
It can be anyone.


01:20:01.330 --> 01:20:04.208
A relative, a lost love.


01:20:04.292 --> 01:20:09.088
When everyone can be found,
we can never lose a friend.


01:20:11.048 --> 01:20:12.884
We can take a suggestion
from the audience.


01:20:12.967 --> 01:20:16.971
- Uh... anyone?
- Find Mercer!


01:20:20.099 --> 01:20:22.518
I'm pretty sure
he doesn't wanna be found.


01:20:23.895 --> 01:20:25.563
- Anyone else?
- Where's Mercer?


01:20:25.646 --> 01:20:29.942
<i>No, Mae, this is what Soul Search
is all about as well as Completion.</i>


01:20:30.026 --> 01:20:31.027
Other suggestions?


01:20:31.110 --> 01:20:32.963
- Let's try Mercer.
- Mercer! Mercer! Mercer!


01:20:32.987 --> 01:20:34.006
<i>It's the whole point
of Soul Search, Mae.</i>


01:20:34.030 --> 01:20:34.864
No.


01:20:34.947 --> 01:20:37.158
Mercer! Mercer!


01:20:37.241 --> 01:20:39.136
- Mercer! Mercer! Mercer!
- No, no, no, no.


01:20:39.160 --> 01:20:40.828
No, I...


01:20:40.912 --> 01:20:43.331
<i>Mae, this is the right choice.</i>


01:20:43.414 --> 01:20:45.708
Wait. Hey, everybody.


01:20:45.791 --> 01:20:47.671
Hey.


01:20:48.210 --> 01:20:50.546
I think it's time we reunited
Mae and Mercer, don't you?


01:20:51.547 --> 01:20:53.758
All right then.
And through the power of the Circle,


01:20:53.841 --> 01:20:56.344
let's bring them
back together again, okay?


01:20:56.427 --> 01:21:00.181
The perfect use of Soul Search.
Let's find your friend.


01:21:02.767 --> 01:21:04.018
Go, Mae, now.


01:21:14.528 --> 01:21:16.989
Okay.


01:21:20.034 --> 01:21:26.207
So our second Soul Search today
involves not a fugitive from justice,


01:21:26.290 --> 01:21:28.709
but you might say a fugitive from, um...


01:21:30.044 --> 01:21:31.044
a friendship.


01:21:39.136 --> 01:21:40.972
This is my friend Mercer.


01:21:42.014 --> 01:21:44.600
I haven't seen him in a while.


01:21:44.684 --> 01:21:45.977
I'd love to see him again.


01:21:47.144 --> 01:21:52.358
Like Fiona, though, he is someone
who is trying not to be found,


01:21:52.441 --> 01:21:54.443
but unlike Fiona, he's not a criminal.


01:21:55.987 --> 01:21:57.154
He's good, people.


01:22:04.954 --> 01:22:06.794
Let's see if we can break
our previous record.


01:22:06.831 --> 01:22:08.499
Whoo!


01:22:29.353 --> 01:22:31.063
Oh, yeah.


01:22:32.064 --> 01:22:33.441
Unbelievable.


01:22:33.524 --> 01:22:35.443
Can you believe that? Yes!


01:22:37.653 --> 01:22:39.196
<i>Do you think he's in here?</i>


01:22:41.991 --> 01:22:43.784
<i>- Mercer!
- Mercer!</i>


01:22:43.868 --> 01:22:44.785
<i>Mercer, are you in there?</i>


01:22:44.869 --> 01:22:47.538
<i>Are you, like, killing deer,
making chandeliers?</i>


01:22:49.457 --> 01:22:52.310
<i>Mercer, we know you're in there.
We see your truck. It's right here.</i>


01:22:52.334 --> 01:22:54.187
<i>- I think he's in there.
- Fine. Let's go around.</i>


01:22:54.211 --> 01:22:57.465
<i>Oh, my God, he's in there!</i>


01:22:57.548 --> 01:22:59.425
<i>Mercer, come out. He's gotta be here.</i>


01:22:59.508 --> 01:23:01.719
Remember to be nice, okay?


01:23:01.802 --> 01:23:03.122
<i>Mercer, we know you're in there.</i>


01:23:03.179 --> 01:23:05.014
You found where he is. You did it.


01:23:05.097 --> 01:23:06.348
I think we should leave.


01:23:06.432 --> 01:23:09.060
<i>No, Mae, stay on this.</i>


01:23:09.143 --> 01:23:11.228
<i>You've got millions of people
enjoying this.</i>


01:23:11.312 --> 01:23:12.980
<i>There he is. There he is.</i>


01:23:13.064 --> 01:23:15.816
<i>There he is. We just got Mercer.
I cannot believe this.</i>


01:23:15.900 --> 01:23:17.620
<i>There he is! He's in the driveway!</i>


01:23:24.492 --> 01:23:26.577
<i>Leave me alone! Jesus Christ!</i>


01:23:48.015 --> 01:23:49.433
You're kidding me.


01:23:53.854 --> 01:23:56.440
Can someone please get me audio
on the camera?


01:23:58.150 --> 01:23:59.568
Mercer.


01:23:59.652 --> 01:24:02.613
Mercer, stop driving. It's just me.


01:24:02.696 --> 01:24:04.323
Mercer, stop the truck.


01:24:04.406 --> 01:24:06.534
You're surrounded by friends.


01:24:07.910 --> 01:24:09.662
Can we turn the speaker up?


01:24:10.913 --> 01:24:11.914
Mercer.


01:24:13.415 --> 01:24:14.583
Can you hear me?


01:24:15.835 --> 01:24:17.253
Mercer, it's me.


01:24:17.336 --> 01:24:18.976
You're surrounded by friends.


01:24:22.591 --> 01:24:24.552
Mercer, stop.


01:24:47.783 --> 01:24:49.451
How you doing, sweetie?


01:24:51.954 --> 01:24:54.582
You've been in bed for three days.


01:24:55.749 --> 01:24:58.586
Come out in the garden
with me today, okay?


01:24:58.669 --> 01:25:02.006
I've got some new snapdragons
that need to be planted.


01:25:03.299 --> 01:25:04.300
Okay?


01:25:05.509 --> 01:25:06.510
Honey.


01:25:09.763 --> 01:25:10.764
Oh, honey.


01:25:12.516 --> 01:25:14.101
Oh.


01:25:15.978 --> 01:25:18.522
Oh.


01:25:18.606 --> 01:25:21.126
<i>The California
Department of Motor Vehicles</i>


01:25:21.150 --> 01:25:23.777
<i>has proposed regulation
that self-driving cars</i>


01:25:23.861 --> 01:25:26.822
<i>should be required to have
a licensed driver inside.</i>


01:25:26.906 --> 01:25:28.324
<i>Is that a good principle?</i>


01:25:28.407 --> 01:25:31.243
<i>Yeah, I mean, I think obviously,</i>


01:25:31.327 --> 01:25:33.829
<i>where technology is today
that is a good principle,</i>


01:25:33.913 --> 01:25:36.308
<i>and of course, we would not suggest
putting something unsafe on the road.</i>


01:25:36.332 --> 01:25:37.791
<i>That's why we have fed...</i>


01:27:33.991 --> 01:27:34.991
<i>Mae.</i>


01:27:36.035 --> 01:27:37.411
Where are you?


01:27:37.494 --> 01:27:40.205
- Are we alone?
- Yes.


01:27:40.289 --> 01:27:41.290
Are we?


01:27:41.373 --> 01:27:42.374
Yes.


01:27:44.585 --> 01:27:47.546
How is it daytime where you are?


01:27:47.629 --> 01:27:50.007
Wait, you're in Scotland.


01:27:50.090 --> 01:27:52.593
Yep, I went home. Can you see?


01:27:54.553 --> 01:27:56.013
What happened?


01:27:56.096 --> 01:27:58.891
What, besides almost ODing
on speed and Soylent?


01:27:58.974 --> 01:28:00.726
Well, I found out I'm allergic to wheat.


01:28:04.438 --> 01:28:07.316
Um... I don't know.


01:28:07.399 --> 01:28:08.692
I guess I almost died?


01:28:12.488 --> 01:28:14.448
Oh, come on, no, don't.
Don't make me cry.


01:28:14.531 --> 01:28:16.950
Come on. No.
I'm not going to cry anymore.


01:28:19.161 --> 01:28:20.201
You getting better there?


01:28:21.246 --> 01:28:23.290
<i>Yeah, I am.</i>


01:28:23.374 --> 01:28:29.004
I eat. I look out the window.
I come out here, and I think I daydream.


01:28:29.088 --> 01:28:30.172
Hadn't done that in years.


01:28:31.673 --> 01:28:32.758
I'm glad you called.


01:28:35.886 --> 01:28:37.388
I didn't want to lose you, too.


01:28:38.931 --> 01:28:39.932
<i>I know.</i>


01:28:41.725 --> 01:28:44.019
Listen, Mae, I'm really,
really sorry about Mercer.


01:28:45.062 --> 01:28:47.898
And I'm a shit friend.
I should've said something sooner.


01:28:47.981 --> 01:28:49.358
I should've, and I'm sorry.


01:28:51.110 --> 01:28:52.319
It's just so horrible.


01:28:54.988 --> 01:28:56.490
Have they spoken to you about it?


01:28:57.825 --> 01:28:58.826
Not yet.


01:28:59.993 --> 01:29:01.620
I'm going in tomorrow.


01:29:01.703 --> 01:29:04.957
<i>Uh-huh. They ask for forgiveness
and never for permission.</i>


01:29:05.040 --> 01:29:07.126
They have to go.
They've broken a hundred laws.


01:29:07.209 --> 01:29:10.212
They've made me suffer
a thousand regulations.


01:29:10.295 --> 01:29:11.839
There's got to be a way to...


01:29:11.922 --> 01:29:14.174
What? Stop people doing bad shit?


01:29:14.258 --> 01:29:16.301
Yeah.


01:29:17.678 --> 01:29:20.889
Do you need some time away here, too?
I've got an extra bed if you want it.


01:29:25.436 --> 01:29:29.356
This feels so good, just being with you.


01:29:29.440 --> 01:29:31.984
- Even like this.
- Yeah.


01:29:33.110 --> 01:29:34.319
Yeah. I know.


01:29:37.614 --> 01:29:39.825
This is going to sound crazy. But...


01:29:39.908 --> 01:29:41.952
I keep having this feeling that...


01:29:42.995 --> 01:29:45.372
Mercer would still be here
if we hadn't lost touch.


01:29:45.456 --> 01:29:47.416
No, come on,
what were you supposed to do?


01:29:47.499 --> 01:29:49.793
I mean, you know, he drifted away.


01:29:50.794 --> 01:29:51.920
It happens.


01:29:52.004 --> 01:29:55.090
Yes, but we have this technology,
all this power.


01:29:55.174 --> 01:29:58.552
Well, I mean, technology
didn't exactly help the situation.


01:30:00.554 --> 01:30:01.555
What if it could?


01:30:07.936 --> 01:30:10.814
Hey, your dad woke up at 4:00
this morning,


01:30:10.898 --> 01:30:12.733
and your light was still on.


01:30:12.816 --> 01:30:15.402
What were you doing in your room?
Were you on the phone?


01:30:17.154 --> 01:30:18.739
It helped.


01:30:18.822 --> 01:30:20.657
I have friends all over the world.


01:30:22.075 --> 01:30:23.076
They care.


01:30:23.160 --> 01:30:24.494
Mm.


01:30:24.578 --> 01:30:28.165
- I spoke to Annie.
- How is she?


01:30:28.248 --> 01:30:29.875
Good. Better.


01:30:31.043 --> 01:30:32.711
I realized how much I missed her.


01:30:37.007 --> 01:30:38.759
I have to go back.


01:30:38.842 --> 01:30:40.260
Go back where?


01:30:42.679 --> 01:30:43.805
Not to the Circle.


01:30:43.889 --> 01:30:45.933
The Circle didn't kill Mercer, Mom.


01:30:46.016 --> 01:30:48.560
It was a bad tool, a bad system.


01:30:48.644 --> 01:30:51.563
It... It can be fixed.


01:30:53.023 --> 01:30:55.609
We're not gonna become Amish, Mom.


01:30:55.692 --> 01:30:58.320
I... When someone dies in a plane crash,


01:30:58.403 --> 01:31:02.324
you don't abandon planes,
you make them safer.


01:31:02.407 --> 01:31:04.076
Mae, at least wait a bit.


01:31:04.159 --> 01:31:06.036
I'm the only one who can do this.


01:31:07.537 --> 01:31:08.538
I'm sorry.


01:31:10.207 --> 01:31:11.708
I love you.


01:31:35.232 --> 01:31:37.526
<i>- Hello?
- Hey, it's me.</i>


01:31:37.609 --> 01:31:40.487
<i>Mae. How are you holding up?</i>


01:31:40.570 --> 01:31:43.532
<i>Better, actually.
I'm coming in to see them today.</i>


01:31:43.615 --> 01:31:46.952
<i>- But I need your help.
- Sure. Name it, anything you need.</i>


01:32:16.648 --> 01:32:19.043
We were worried
when we didn't hear from you for so long.


01:32:19.067 --> 01:32:20.819
We were very concerned.


01:32:20.902 --> 01:32:23.196
No contact for four days.
Your camera was off.


01:32:23.280 --> 01:32:24.280
How are you doing?


01:32:27.409 --> 01:32:28.409
I'm okay.


01:32:29.494 --> 01:32:31.514
Tom and I have been talking
about how little we knew


01:32:31.538 --> 01:32:33.457
about this young man Mercer.


01:32:33.540 --> 01:32:35.751
He's someone we should
have been able to help.


01:32:35.834 --> 01:32:38.587
A fund should be established
for his family.


01:32:39.713 --> 01:32:42.215
Yeah, absolutely, that's done.


01:32:42.299 --> 01:32:45.260
Listen, Mae, we would like
to offer you a new role


01:32:45.344 --> 01:32:50.015
here at the Circle
with no formal obligation,


01:32:50.098 --> 01:32:52.059
maybe a lighter schedule.


01:32:52.142 --> 01:32:54.644
You can choose your own projects,
and you can think long-term.


01:32:54.728 --> 01:32:57.064
You could take pottery, if you wanted.


01:32:57.147 --> 01:32:58.666
I don't know if you're aware of this,


01:32:58.690 --> 01:33:01.443
- but one of our founders, Ty Lafitte...
- Yeah.


01:33:01.526 --> 01:33:06.823
...was given the same opportunity,
and now he loves his freedom.


01:33:06.907 --> 01:33:10.118
And I don't know
if this is any consolation,


01:33:10.202 --> 01:33:13.580
but at our next Dream Friday...


01:33:13.663 --> 01:33:15.540
We're going to imagine a world


01:33:15.624 --> 01:33:19.336
in which every disturbed young man
could be kept safe.


01:33:21.046 --> 01:33:22.339
Would you like to join us?


01:33:24.466 --> 01:33:27.094
I'm not sure I'm ready for that. But...


01:33:29.054 --> 01:33:30.138
thanks for the invitation.


01:33:31.139 --> 01:33:32.139
Take your time.


01:33:57.791 --> 01:34:01.169
I did what you asked.
You won't believe the shit I found.


01:34:01.253 --> 01:34:05.674
It's worse than I could've imagined.
And, and look, Mae, I'm so sorry.


01:34:05.757 --> 01:34:09.636
I never met him, but they should build
a monument to Mercer.


01:34:11.888 --> 01:34:15.559
- Yeah.
- I mean, this... this changes everything.


01:34:15.642 --> 01:34:18.687
Okay, he's the martyr
who will wake everyone up, right?


01:34:18.770 --> 01:34:22.232
I don't know what you're planning,
but if you turn that camera back on,


01:34:22.315 --> 01:34:25.235
whatever you say about the Circle
is heard around the world,


01:34:25.318 --> 01:34:26.945
and it changes everything.


01:34:42.335 --> 01:34:43.335
<i>Hello</i>.


01:34:47.007 --> 01:34:48.008
Hello again.


01:34:53.722 --> 01:34:55.056
<i>- Hi!
- Hey, Mae.</i>


01:35:04.524 --> 01:35:06.234
And that is how it should have been,


01:35:06.318 --> 01:35:08.820
how it could've been,
but we didn't have the technology.


01:35:08.904 --> 01:35:10.155
But we will.


01:35:10.238 --> 01:35:11.615
And when we do,


01:35:11.698 --> 01:35:14.993
when every vehicle
is known and connected,


01:35:15.076 --> 01:35:18.413
the data we have will help us
improve traffic patterns,


01:35:18.497 --> 01:35:25.086
keep drivers safe, and optimize
Circle services across all platforms.


01:35:25.170 --> 01:35:28.298
Shopping. Lodging.


01:35:28.381 --> 01:35:29.925
Attractions.


01:35:30.967 --> 01:35:32.636
Attractions like Mae Holland.


01:35:32.719 --> 01:35:34.399
Mae, come on out here.


01:35:34.471 --> 01:35:36.848
<i>Come on out here. Gosh, Mae.</i>


01:35:36.932 --> 01:35:39.267
<i>Oh, you're transparent again.
Look at that.</i>


01:35:39.351 --> 01:35:41.520
Hello, hello.


01:35:41.603 --> 01:35:42.603
Mae Holland.


01:35:42.646 --> 01:35:46.149
Welcome back, Mae!


01:35:46.233 --> 01:35:51.613
Now, Mae is a brave person


01:35:51.696 --> 01:35:53.532
because even amid her grieving,


01:35:53.615 --> 01:35:58.453
she knows that she has a responsibility
to her followers,


01:35:58.537 --> 01:36:00.080
to the whole world, really.


01:36:01.665 --> 01:36:06.086
Thanks for saying that, Eamon.
And I couldn't agree with you more.


01:36:07.504 --> 01:36:10.423
I know that Tom, he agrees, too.


01:36:10.507 --> 01:36:14.594
Right, Tom? You want to come out?


01:36:14.678 --> 01:36:17.305
- Come on.
- There you are.


01:36:21.268 --> 01:36:24.771
First, I want to thank everyone here
and around the world


01:36:24.854 --> 01:36:28.191
for the support you've given me
since we lost Mercer.


01:36:28.275 --> 01:36:33.029
These past days,
I've realized something about myself


01:36:33.113 --> 01:36:34.281
and about the Circle.


01:36:35.448 --> 01:36:37.784
It's that I don't want to be disconnected.


01:36:39.202 --> 01:36:43.164
I don't want to have to search
for friends, family, my loved ones.


01:36:44.374 --> 01:36:45.875
We lost track of Mercer.


01:36:47.419 --> 01:36:50.005
But what if we never lost touch
with each other?


01:36:52.215 --> 01:36:54.009
What if instead of searching...


01:36:55.343 --> 01:36:56.511
we always knew?


01:36:58.638 --> 01:37:03.560
What I am proposing is radical openness
and unbroken connection.


01:37:06.313 --> 01:37:08.273
It would have to start here.


01:37:08.356 --> 01:37:10.025
Mm-hm.


01:37:10.108 --> 01:37:13.486
You know the usual hypocrisy
of the digital world.


01:37:13.570 --> 01:37:17.741
We want everything in the Cloud,
and yet our leaders...


01:37:19.701 --> 01:37:21.536
live above it.


01:37:21.620 --> 01:37:26.249
So I want to invite Eamon and Tom


01:37:26.333 --> 01:37:30.712
to join me in this experiment
that they've pioneered.


01:37:32.339 --> 01:37:38.094
I invite them to set an example
for the Circle and the world...


01:37:41.181 --> 01:37:42.724
to go transparent.


01:37:47.437 --> 01:37:49.022
What do you say, guys?


01:37:51.024 --> 01:37:52.025
Will you join me?


01:37:59.157 --> 01:38:03.161
Wouldn't it be great to know
their every move, their every word?


01:38:03.244 --> 01:38:07.624
From now on,
every message, meeting, phone call,


01:38:07.707 --> 01:38:12.962
all will be public,
knowable, never erased.


01:38:14.130 --> 01:38:16.299
In fact, Ty Lafitte has helped us
make every document


01:38:16.383 --> 01:38:20.011
in the company's history public.


01:38:20.095 --> 01:38:22.639
Every message, every plan including...


01:38:22.722 --> 01:38:25.975
And this is my favorite part,
every e-mail,


01:38:26.059 --> 01:38:30.105
from Tom and Eamon's accounts,
and their private accounts...


01:38:31.106 --> 01:38:33.292
...and their secondary
confidential accounts.


01:38:33.316 --> 01:38:37.278
And even their super-secret
scrambled code accounts,


01:38:37.362 --> 01:38:43.284
that nobody, not even their assistants
or wives knew existed.


01:38:44.786 --> 01:38:45.912
Until now.


01:38:48.873 --> 01:38:50.750
It's all been sent to you already.


01:38:54.421 --> 01:38:56.589
We are so fucked.


01:38:56.673 --> 01:39:00.593
I think we're entitled
to that kind of access. Don't you?


01:39:00.677 --> 01:39:04.097
Yeah.


01:39:05.223 --> 01:39:07.392
Mae, you are a very brave woman
to do this.


01:39:13.273 --> 01:39:16.359
There are no more secrets.


01:39:16.443 --> 01:39:18.194
Privacy was a temporary thing.


01:39:19.404 --> 01:39:20.488
And now it's over.


01:39:22.240 --> 01:39:24.409
We won't live in the shadows anymore.


01:39:25.827 --> 01:39:28.037
Hey. Good timing.


01:39:47.599 --> 01:39:48.600
Thank you.


01:39:49.851 --> 01:39:51.519
Thank you.


01:39:51.603 --> 01:39:58.568
I'm tired of hiding,
these passwords and secret knocks,


01:39:58.651 --> 01:40:01.446
the difference between public and private.


01:40:03.072 --> 01:40:04.115
Aren't you all?


01:40:05.533 --> 01:40:07.494
Come to think of it,


01:40:07.577 --> 01:40:09.496
I'm tired of standing here in the dark.


01:40:11.456 --> 01:40:12.665
Don't worry.


01:40:12.749 --> 01:40:17.545
Eamon will catch up. Or maybe he won't.


01:40:17.629 --> 01:40:20.381
Either way, it's time for a change.


01:40:20.465 --> 01:40:22.383
We're gonna remake this place.


01:40:22.467 --> 01:40:28.056
And if we do it right,
we will set an example for everyone.


01:40:28.139 --> 01:40:29.724
The future won't wait.


